window.XEVO_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"title":"xevolution documentation","url":"index.html","text":"xevolution documentation xevolution is a Python library for evolutionary computation. An application defines its candidates, evaluation, and variation operators; xevolution manages the population, selection, identities, history, and checkpoint continuation. This documentation covers version 0.1.1, including all three example applications. Start here If you are new to the package, read the overview for the central concepts and a walkthrough of one evolutionary run. Then choose an example from the examples guide. Each example has its own explanation of the genotype, fitness, operators, configuration, commands, and results. Example What it demonstrates Guide Arithmetic expression search Evolve a bit string, decode its expression, and score its distance from a target Arithmetic expressions Shared-resource choices Evaluate the whole population when each candidate's score depends on other candidates Shared resources Digits neural architecture search Evolve hidden layers and activation, train a fresh MLP, and use validation accuracy as fitness Digits MLP The arithmetic and shared-resource examples use only the Python standard library. The Digits example uses the optional neural dependency group and runs on CPU. Every Python dependency belongs in a project-local virtual environment managed by uv. Use the library Question Documentation What does xevolution do, and how does a run work? Package overview Which example should I start with? Three examples How do I change population size, selection, operators, or stopping? JSON configuration How do I run, pause, resume, inspect, or analyze an experiment? Command-line reference How do I supply my own genotype and evaluator? Application extension contracts Which module owns each responsibility? Architecture and ownership What must be saved for reliable continuation? Checkpointing and recovery Commands in these guides run from the source project root, containing pyproject.toml, uv.lock, and examples/. This documentation archive can be read independently; it does not contain the installable package or application source files. Use it alongside the full xevolution Python project. Understand the port and its verification The generic framework was ported from the Java Xnet xevolution sub-project. The new design uses Python composition, typed records, explicit JSON state, and application-defined extension contracts. NEAT, the graphical interface, the operator workbench, and compatibility with old Java runs are outside the agreed scope. Reference Contents Porting notes Java-to-Python mappings, utility migration, and deliberate algorithm corrections Python release verification Executed 0.1.1 checks, installed-wheel tests, checkpoint tests, and measured Digits results Full port specification Agreed behavior, module design, algorithms, persistence, and acceptance criteria Contributor and agent guidelines Repository practices, uv environment policy, typing, testing, and state ownership Maintain and rebuild these docs The Markdown/HTML layout, offline HTML generation, validation, and source maintenance Read either edition The Markdown edition starts at docs/markdown/index.md; the HTML edition starts at docs/html/index.html. The HTML edition includes local navigation, section links, highlighted code, and a search index. All reading assets are bundled, so opening it does not require a web server or an internet connection. External source references remain ordinary links to their original websites. Both editions share the same Markdown content. Edit the Markdown and rebuild the HTML using the documentation build instructions. The bundle retains the project's license text, accompanying GPL text, and source notice.","order":0},{"title":"xevolution: what it does and how it works","url":"overview.html","text":"xevolution: what it does and how it works xevolution is a Python library for searching a space of candidate solutions through evolutionary computation. You describe a candidate, define how to evaluate it, and provide ways to create variations. The library maintains a population, selects parents, produces new candidates, evaluates them, and keeps the history needed to understand and continue the search. The same engine can search for an arithmetic expression, distribute competing candidates across shared resources, or choose the architecture of a neural network. Those are the three included examples. The framework does not need to know what their genotypes mean. The package is headless: it runs through a Python API or command line without a graphical interface. It supports Python 3.12 and later. Its core has no runtime dependencies outside the standard library. Application code can use numerical libraries, simulators, or other services through the evaluation contracts. The Digits example adds its numerical dependencies through an optional uv dependency group. The central ideas An evolutionary run repeatedly asks two questions: how well does each current candidate perform, and which variations should be tried next? The vocabulary below separates the application problem from the engine's bookkeeping. Term Meaning in xevolution Example Genotype The application-defined description that operators change An immutable bit string, an integer resource choice, or an MLP architecture Individual A genotype wrapped with a unique framework ID, birth generation, parent IDs, and observations Individual 37 carries a three-layer neural architecture Population An ordered collection of individuals evaluated together Eight candidate architectures in the normal Digits preset Evaluation Application work that measures a candidate or a whole population Decode an expression, calculate resource sharing, or train and validate a network Fitness One finite scalar used as the optimization objective Validation accuracy of 0.945 Metric An additional named numerical observation Parameter count, expression error, or resource crowding Mutation An operation that produces a child from one parent genotype Resize one neural layer Crossover An operation that produces a child from two parent genotypes Combine the layer descriptions of two architectures Generation A completed population and its accepted observations Generation 0 is the evaluated initial population A genotype is not necessarily the object that evaluation executes. In the Digits example it describes layers and activation; the evaluator constructs a network and trains its weights. In the arithmetic example, the evaluator decodes a bit string before calculating a score. Any such decoding or model construction belongs to the application. The built-in comparison policies and historical-best tracking treat larger fitness as better. Finite negative values and zero are valid. To minimize a cost, an application can return its negative or another explicitly chosen quality score. Choose a compatible selector: tournament selection accepts signed fitness, while direct roulette selection requires nonnegative weights. See configuration for the supported policies. Metrics do not silently become extra objectives. For example, the Digits application records parameter count but ranks candidates by accuracy alone. An unevaluated fitness is represented separately from zero, and missing metrics remain missing in statistical summaries. What the application supplies An Experiment composes the application-specific pieces. Small functions, callable objects, and immutable configuration records work together through typed structural contracts; an application does not need a framework base class hierarchy. Application contribution Responsibility Initializer Create a genotype using the run-owned random generator Individual or population evaluator Return fitness and optional metrics Mutation and/or crossover operators Produce valid children while leaving parents unchanged Clone policy Preserve independent offspring and retained observations, or share explicitly immutable data Payload codec Describe how genotype data is encoded and restored for checkpoints Optional policies and hooks Customize parent selection, survival, stopping, and adaptive updates Explicit component state Preserve any application information that affects later behavior The engine owns IDs, population order, generation numbers, evaluation counts, birth records, and the completed-generation boundary. It does not require arbitrary genotypes to support comparison, hashing, indexing, or truth testing. Two individuals can carry identical genotypes and still have distinct IDs and separate evaluation histories. JSON configuration names an importable experiment factory, such as examples.neural_digits:build_experiment. The factory receives validated core configuration, resolved settings, and the managed random streams. It validates its own application parameters and constructs an Experiment. This makes the same application reconstructible during resume. The extension guide explains the exact callable signatures, ownership rules, codecs, and state contracts. What happens in one run? Generation 0 is meaningful work. On a fresh run the engine resolves settings, constructs components, initializes genotypes, assigns IDs, and evaluates the initial population. It then performs the same finalization steps used by later generations: validation, statistics, adaptive updates, stopping, and optional checkpoint publication. Each later transition follows this order: Choose survivors. The configured survivor policy decides which previous individuals, if any, remain in the next population. Produce the remaining members. Parents are selected from the complete previous population. Each required child follows either the mutation route or the crossover route. Operators receive the managed random generator and return a new genotype; the framework assigns its ID and parent links. Evaluate the assembled population. All members are evaluated, including retained survivors. The engine validates the complete set of results before accepting fitness values or incrementing accepted-evaluation counts. Record observations and prepare the next transition. Statistics and independent historical-best snapshots are updated. Registered adaptive hooks may update their explicit state and settings for the next transition. Decide whether to stop. The stopping policy runs once against the finalized state. Its decision and any stateful stopping window are retained. Commit the completed generation. If persistence is enabled, scientific history and the checkpoint are committed before the engine exposes success. Parent selection and survival answer different questions. An individual that will not survive can still contribute offspring to the transition replacing it. Conversely, retaining an elite preserves its identity and genotype, but does not reuse its old fitness without reevaluation. The mutation_probability setting chooses mutation or crossover for each child. It does not request a second mutation stage after crossover. Operators can declare a defined inapplicability condition, and reproduction uses explicit fallbacks and finite retry budgets. Unexpected programming errors remain failures. The default survivor policy replaces the entire population. Other supplied policies include elitism, and applications can supply custom policies. The architecture guide describes the modules responsible for each stage; configuration describes settings and stopping conditions. Independent and whole-population evaluation Use an individual evaluator when a candidate's score can be calculated on its own. Arithmetic expressions and the Digits networks use this hook. The engine adapts it into a complete population evaluation and provides a stable evaluation context for each individual. Use a population evaluator when scoring needs the population together. In the shared-resource example, a candidate receives its chosen resource's quality divided by the number of candidates choosing that resource. The same genotype can receive different fitness when its neighbors change. A population evaluator returns results associated with individual IDs. Every expected ID must occur exactly once, with no extra IDs and no non-finite scores. Results are applied in the engine's stable population order. If both hooks are supplied, the population hook takes precedence; candidates are not evaluated twice. These hooks define what the application computes. They do not imply that the core schedules parallel workers or supplies a simulator. An application can place such work behind its evaluator while satisfying the result and state contracts. History, checkpoints, and continuation A persistent run contains versioned JSON configuration and checkpoint state, a JSON Lines scientific history, and any codec attachments. History records population observations, statistics, births, parent links, operator details, settings, and stopping decisions. The recorded historical best is the best fitness observation seen; for a population-dependent problem, interpreting that observation also requires its evaluation context. The core inspect and analyze commands read committed results. Analysis reconstructs summaries and genealogy without evaluating candidates or advancing the run. The Digits companion report is a distinct application command that explicitly performs two fresh training runs for its final comparison. Checkpoints describe completed generations. They preserve population payloads, IDs and counters, current and next-transition settings, random generator states, historical state, application component state, stopping state, and the committed history boundary. Resume reconstructs the application and restores that state without reinitializing or reevaluating the saved generation. Exact continuation requires compatible code, data, numerical execution, and complete future-affecting state. An interrupted, uncommitted generation may run again after a crash. A saved terminal decision stays terminal under normal resume; an operational pause leaves the configured stopping policy intact. Read checkpointing and recovery before relying on continuation for an application with external side effects or stateful evaluators. Run an example Use the full project's root directory, containing pyproject.toml, uv.lock, and examples/. The documentation archive can be read independently, but the commands need the source project. All Python dependencies belong in a local virtual environment managed by uv. Keep UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT unset or pointing to this project's .venv. uv python install 3.12 uv sync --locked --group dev uv run --locked python -m xevolution run --config examples/arithmetic.json --output runs/arithmetic uv run --locked python -m xevolution inspect runs/arithmetic uv run --locked python -m xevolution analyze runs/arithmetic --output reports/arithmetic Use a new or empty output directory for a fresh run. The arithmetic preset stops when it finds its target or reaches its generation limit, so a successful initial population can end the run at generation 0. The arithmetic guide explains its encoding and fitness. To embed a configured application in Python, the same engine is available through the public API: from xevolution import create_engine with create_engine(\"examples/batch_problem.json\") as engine: result = engine.run() best_fitness = None if result.best is None else result.best.fitness print(result.generation, best_fitness) This call reads the configuration file and keeps run state in memory because no output directory was supplied. Add output=\"runs/shared-resources\" to create_engine when a persistent run is wanted. Applications can also construct Experiment and RunConfig directly; see extensions. Where to go next Goal Read next Choose a working example Examples overview Understand representation, decoding, and simple scoring Arithmetic expressions Evaluate interactions between candidates Shared resources Train a model inside fitness evaluation Digits MLP architecture evolution Change populations, selection, operators, or stopping JSON configuration Run, pause, inspect, resume, or export results Command-line reference Build an application Application extension contracts Understand library internals Architecture and ownership Design reliable saved runs Checkpointing and recovery Return to the documentation index.","order":1},{"title":"Example projects","url":"examples.html","text":"Example projects The three examples use the same evolutionary engine with different representations and evaluation tasks. Start with arithmetic to see the basic contracts, continue with shared resources to understand joint evaluation, and use Digits to explore architecture evolution with a training step inside the evaluator. The package overview introduces the common lifecycle. Choose an example Example Genotype Fitness Evaluation hook Main lesson Arithmetic expressions An immutable string of bits encoding operands and operators 1 / (1 + absolute_error); invalid division receives zero Individual Separate a representation from its decoded behavior, then vary and score it Shared resources An integer identifying a resource Resource quality divided by current occupancy Whole population A candidate's score can depend on the other candidates present Digits MLP Hidden-layer widths and an activation function Fraction of validation images classified correctly Individual Evolve architecture while ordinary training learns each candidate's weights Each application provides initialization, mutation, crossover, cloning, and an explicit genotype codec. The engine supplies IDs, evaluation accounting, selection, generations, stopping, genealogy, and checkpoints. The examples are application code in the source project; importing the core package does not import them or start a run. Environment and commands Run commands from the full xevolution project root, beside pyproject.toml and examples/. The standalone documentation archive does not contain an installed application environment. Use the repository-local .venv managed by uv, with UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT unset or pointing to that directory. The arithmetic and shared-resource applications need only the standard library at runtime: uv python install 3.12 uv sync --locked --group dev Digits uses an optional numerical dependency group: uv sync --locked --group dev --group neural Keep --group neural on Digits run, resume, and reporting commands so uv includes the required numerical dependencies. All three applications run on CPU. The core wheel retains an empty runtime dependency list. Arithmetic: a compact first run uv run --locked python -m xevolution run --config examples/arithmetic.json --output runs/arithmetic uv run --locked python -m xevolution inspect runs/arithmetic uv run --locked python -m xevolution analyze runs/arithmetic --output reports/arithmetic The supplied preset seeks the integer value 64 using expressions with ten operands. It uses 100 individuals, direct roulette parent selection, complete replacement, and an equal probability of choosing mutation or crossover. A mutation operation then considers each bit for flipping with probability 0.01. Those two probabilities serve different purposes. The stopping policy ends the run on an exact solution or at completed generation 200. It can stop at generation 0 if an initial candidate is already exact. Expression evaluation is left to right, and integer division truncates toward zero. The full guide explains why a printed expression must be interpreted using those rules. Shared resources: a population that affects its own scores uv run --locked python -m xevolution run --config examples/batch_problem.json --output runs/shared-resources uv run --locked python -m xevolution inspect runs/shared-resources uv run --locked python -m xevolution analyze runs/shared-resources --output reports/shared-resources The preset contains 48 individuals choosing among four resources with qualities [1.0, 1.8, 3.0, 5.0]. It evaluates generations 0 through 12, giving 624 accepted individual results. Each generation uses one joint evaluator call to calculate occupancy and score the whole population. If five candidates choose the resource with quality 5.0, each receives fitness 1.0. If ten choose it, each receives 0.5. This makes the example useful for understanding why fitness cannot always be cached by genotype or calculated without the surrounding population. See the shared-resource guide for operators, metrics, and interpretation of historical best observations. Digits: evolve architecture, train weights, measure accuracy Start with the quick preset: uv run --locked --group neural python -m xevolution run --config examples/neural_digits_quick.json --output runs/digits-quick uv run --locked --group neural python -m examples.digits_report runs/digits-quick Or use the normal preset: uv run --locked --group neural python -m xevolution run --config examples/neural_digits.json --output runs/digits uv run --locked --group neural python -m examples.digits_report runs/digits --output reports/digits.json The genotype allows one to three hidden layers with 8, 16, 32, or 64 neurons per layer, and ReLU or tanh activation. Each candidate gets a fresh model, fixed training recipe, and the same split and training seeds. Accuracy on 400 validation images is the sole evolutionary fitness; final test images stay outside selection. Preset Population Evaluated generations Epochs per candidate Search evaluations Quick 6 0 through 3 10 24 Normal 8 0 through 5 30 48 The companion report requires a terminated run. It trains the selected architecture and a fixed 32-neuron ReLU baseline afresh with the original recipe, reproduces the selected architecture's saved validation score, and evaluates each model on the held-out test set once. These two fits are separate from evolutionary history. See the Digits walkthrough for full details and verification for measured runtime and results. Settings and results shared by all three Application parameters live under experiment.parameters in each JSON configuration. The top-level evolution and termination sections control the framework. Changing a configuration is a way to start a new experiment; resume restores the recorded configuration instead of applying edits made afterward. All supplied presets use one finite scalar fitness and maximize it. Extra metrics are observations, not additional objectives. Generation 0 is included in evaluation counts, and every new population is evaluated in full. In the Digits presets this includes the retained elite, whose architecture is trained afresh in each generation. The core commands have the same meanings for every example: Command Work performed run Create and execute a fresh run using a new or empty output directory resume Continue from an existing completed-generation checkpoint inspect Read settings, state, statistics, and compatibility metadata analyze Export committed generations, observations, and genealogy without evaluating candidates digits_report is an additional application command with an explicit training and test-scoring step. It is not another name for the core analyze operation. To try pause and resume with the shared-resource example: uv run --locked python -m xevolution run --config examples/batch_problem.json --output runs/shared-paused --pause-after-generation 2 uv run --locked python -m xevolution resume runs/shared-paused The pause commits generation 2 and leaves the configured generation-12 stopping policy intact. The Digits guide gives equivalent commands with its optional dependency group. Exact continuation requires compatible code, data, numerical execution, and explicit component state; the full contract is in checkpointing and recovery. Use an example as a starting point Choose arithmetic when developing a new representation and simple independent scoring. Choose shared resources when the evaluator needs interactions or a common population context. Choose Digits when evaluation constructs and trains a temporary model, and the genotype should describe only the choices that evolution changes. Keep the framework's bookkeeping in the engine. Define your genotype's ownership and codec, validate application parameters in the factory, use the supplied random generators, and save every component field that affects future results. The extension guide describes those contracts; the configuration reference and CLI reference cover the reusable controls. Return to the documentation index.","order":2},{"title":"Arithmetic expression search","url":"arithmetic_demo.html","text":"Arithmetic expression search This example evolves bit strings that describe integer arithmetic expressions. The task is to find an expression whose value matches a target, such as 64. It is a compact introduction to the division of work in xevolution: the application defines the representation, operators, and scoring rule; the framework manages populations, selection, identity, history, and checkpoints. Start with the package overview if evolutionary runs are new to you. The examples guide compares this application with the shared-resource and Digits examples. Run the supplied preset The application lives in examples/arithmetic.py, and its configuration is examples/arithmetic.json. Run the following from the project root containing pyproject.toml and examples/, rather than from the documentation directory: uv python install 3.12 uv sync --locked --group dev uv run --locked python -m xevolution run --config examples/arithmetic.json --output runs/arithmetic uv run --locked python -m xevolution inspect runs/arithmetic uv run --locked python -m xevolution analyze runs/arithmetic --output reports/arithmetic All dependencies belong to the repository's uv-managed .venv. Keep UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT unset or pointing to that directory. This example uses the Python standard library and needs no numerical packages, dataset, or GPU. The Python example module is distributed with the source project; it is not installed inside the core xevolution wheel. The run command requires a new or empty output directory. Its JSON summary reports the completed generation, accepted-evaluation count, historical best fitness, and stopping reason. The analysis command writes reports/arithmetic/analysis.json. What the genotype represents A genotype is an immutable string containing only 0 and 1. The first four bits encode an unsigned integer from 0 to 15. Each subsequent six-bit group contains a two-bit operator followed by another four-bit integer. Operator bits Operation 00 Addition, + 01 Subtraction, - 10 Multiplication, * 11 Integer division, / The terms parameter counts the numbers in an expression. There are terms - 1 operators and 4 + 6 * (terms - 1) bits. The supplied ten-term configuration therefore uses 58-bit genotypes. The number of terms remains fixed throughout a run. The decoder evaluates operations from left to right. Division truncates toward zero, including when an intermediate result is negative. For example, 1 - 8 / 3 is evaluated as (1 - 8) / 3, producing -2. A zero divisor makes the entire expression invalid. A small worked example For three terms, the bit string 0111001001100100 decodes as follows: Bits Meaning 0111 Start with 7 00 1001 Add 9 10 0100 Multiply by 4 The result is (7 + 9) * 4 = 64. You can inspect this representation with the application's helper functions: from examples.arithmetic import decode, expression bits = \"0111001001100100\" print(expression(bits)) # 7 + 9 * 4 print(decode(bits)) # 64 The text returned by expression() does not add parentheses. Read it using the example's left-to-right rule; feeding that text to Python's eval() would apply different precedence rules. The example's decoder never uses eval(). How fitness is calculated The individual evaluation hook decodes one candidate and calculates: absolute_error = abs(target - decoded_value) fitness = 1 / (1 + absolute_error) Higher fitness is better. An exact match scores 1; an error of 1 scores 0.5; an error of 3 scores 0.25. An expression containing division by zero scores 0. Its invalidity is an ordinary evaluation outcome, with no infinite or missing fitness value. The evaluator also returns these measurements: Metric Meaning valid 1 for a valid expression; 0 for division by zero value Decoded integer value, recorded as a float; valid expressions only absolute_error Distance from the target; valid expressions only complexity Number of terms, recorded for both valid and invalid expressions Complexity is descriptive and does not affect selection. Every candidate in this example has the same configured term count. Because invalid candidates omit value and absolute_error, the framework's summaries calculate those means only from candidates that supplied the metric. Missing values do not become zeros. Initialization, mutation, and crossover Initialization samples each bit with equal probability using the run-owned random generator. Mutation visits every bit and flips it independently with the application's mutation_rate. It can leave the string unchanged when no bit is selected. Such a child still receives a fresh framework identity and is evaluated normally. Crossover chooses a cut between two bits, takes the prefix from the first parent, and the suffix from the second. A cut may fall inside a number or operator field. The child retains the required bit-string length, and every two-bit/four-bit field still decodes to an operator/operand. Division by zero is handled later by evaluation. Parents are unchanged. The clone function may return the same string because strings are immutable. The explicit BitStringCodec checks the alphabet and configured length when encoding or restoring a genotype. Two probability settings have different jobs: Setting Supplied value Effect evolution.mutation_probability 0.5 Choose mutation or crossover for each child experiment.parameters.mutation_rate 0.01 Chance of flipping each bit after the mutation route is chosen The framework does not apply an additional mutation stage after crossover. See configuration and extension contracts for the general routing and operator rules. Configuration The application's namespace is experiment.parameters: Parameter Default and supplied value Accepted values target 64 An integer representable as a finite floating-point metric terms 10 An integer from 1 through 32 mutation_rate 0.01 A finite number from 0 through 1 Booleans are not accepted as numeric parameters. Unknown parameter names are errors. Some targets cannot be represented by a particular term count, so a bounded stopping policy remains useful even when a target score is configured. The supplied framework settings are: Setting Value Evolutionary seed 12345 Population size 100 Parent selection Roulette using the nonnegative fitness values Survivor policy Complete replacement Maximum attempts per child 100 Termination Stop at generation 200, or earlier when fitness reaches 1 Generation 0 is the evaluated initial population. An exact target can already appear there, allowing the run to finish after just 100 accepted evaluations. A run reaching generation 200 would evaluate 201 populations, or 20,100 candidates. Complete replacement gives every child a new ID, while genealogy and retained history preserve its relationships to earlier generations. During documentation verification, the supplied preset reached the exact target in generation 0, with 100 accepted evaluations and fitness 1. If your run finishes immediately with that result, the target was already present in the initial population. The longer-run variation below makes the generation cycle easier to observe. Read the results and observe a longer run Look at best_fitness in the run summary and the best record from inspect. A value of 1 means that the target was reached. The inspected best record also contains the bit-string payload and its value and absolute_error metrics when valid. The generic CLI reports the stored representation; application helpers such as expression() can turn a bit string into readable arithmetic. The analysis JSON contains generation summaries, individual birth and observation records, and genealogy edges. The flipped_positions and cut_position details supplied by the operators explain how children were created. Analysis does not decode or evaluate the candidates again. For a demonstration that always includes several generations, save a copy of the preset as examples/arithmetic_progress.json and replace its termination object with: {\"max_generations\": 20} This runs for the chosen generation budget even if an exact expression appears early. You can then pause that new run and continue it: uv run --locked python -m xevolution run --config examples/arithmetic_progress.json --output runs/arithmetic-progress --pause-after-generation 2 uv run --locked python -m xevolution resume runs/arithmetic-progress Resume restores the saved configuration and completed-generation state. It does not use later edits to the original JSON file, and it does not extend a run that has already met its stopping condition. See checkpointing for compatibility and recovery requirements and the CLI reference for operational pause behavior. Useful next steps Change the target, bit-mutation rate, or population size in a new configuration and compare the recorded histories. To evolve expression length itself, extend the genotype, operators, and codec together; changing only the decoder would leave inconsistent ownership and persistence rules. The shared-resource example introduces scores that depend on the other population members. The Digits example keeps the individual hook but makes evaluation train a neural network before scoring it.","order":3},{"title":"Shared resources and population evaluation","url":"batch_demo.html","text":"Shared resources and population evaluation This example models candidates choosing among resources whose quality must be shared. A candidate receives a larger share when fewer population members choose the same resource. Its fitness therefore depends on both its own choice and the choices made by the rest of the population. The example demonstrates xevolution's whole-population evaluation hook. The framework passes the complete population to the application, which computes all the related scores together and returns them with individual IDs. The package overview explains where evaluation fits into a run; the examples guide compares the three applications. Run the supplied preset The application is examples/batch_problem.py, configured by examples/batch_problem.json. Run these commands from the project root containing pyproject.toml and the examples/ package: uv python install 3.12 uv sync --locked --group dev uv run --locked python -m xevolution run --config examples/batch_problem.json --output runs/shared-resources uv run --locked python -m xevolution inspect runs/shared-resources uv run --locked python -m xevolution analyze runs/shared-resources --output reports/shared-resources The project uses a local virtual environment managed by uv. Keep UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT unset or pointing to its .venv. This application uses only the Python standard library; it needs no external dataset or numerical package. The example factory is supplied by the source project, rather than installed inside the core wheel. The output run directory must be new or empty. The analysis command writes reports/shared-resources/analysis.json. It reads the committed history and does not run another population evaluation. Genotype and fitness Each genotype is one integer: a zero-based index into the configured resource_quality list. The preset contains four resources: Resource index Quality 0 1.0 1 1.8 2 3.0 3 5.0 For each resource, the application counts how many population members chose it. Every candidate at that resource receives the same share: fitness = resource_quality[choice] / occupancy[choice] The highest-quality resource is not automatically the best choice in a particular population. For example, quality 5 shared by ten candidates gives each fitness 0.5; quality 1.8 shared by two gives each fitness 0.9. Each result also contains two metrics: Metric Meaning resource The candidate's chosen resource index, recorded as a float crowding The number of candidates sharing that resource, including itself All configured qualities are positive and finite. Every evaluated candidate contributes to the count at its own resource, so the denominator is at least one. Resources with no occupants contribute no individual evaluations. A small worked example Consider two resources with qualities [6.0, 2.0] and three candidates. These qualities are chosen to make the arithmetic clear; they differ from the four-resource preset. Candidate label First choice First fitness Choice after B moves New fitness A 0 6 / 2 = 3 0 6 / 1 = 6 B 0 6 / 2 = 3 1 2 / 2 = 1 C 1 2 / 1 = 2 1 2 / 2 = 1 Only B changed its choice, yet all three scores changed. A's unchanged genotype cannot be assigned a correct score without knowing the population. The letters label two hypothetical population arrangements. They are separate from resource indices and literal framework IDs; an actual evolutionary child receives a new integer ID. How the application uses the framework build_experiment() supplies evaluate_population=application.evaluate_population. It does not supply the individual evaluate hook. For every generation the population evaluator: Counts resource occupancy using the entire population. Calculates each candidate's share and descriptive metrics. Returns one (individual_id, EvaluationResult) pair for every individual. The implementation deliberately returns these pairs in reverse population order. xevolution associates them by explicit ID, validates complete coverage, and applies accepted results in its deterministic population order. Applications must not assume that the position of a result identifies its candidate. This is joint scoring performed once for the complete population. The framework does not also call an individual evaluator or count accepted evaluations twice. A population of 48 still contributes 48 accepted individual evaluations to the framework's counter, even though one population-hook call produced all 48 results. See evaluation contracts for result validation and the choice between the two hooks. Initialization and variation Initialization chooses a resource uniformly with the run-owned random generator. Mutation also chooses uniformly from all resource indices, so it can select the current resource again. Crossover chooses one of the two parents' resource indices with equal probability. This representation has one gene; crossover therefore inherits a complete parental choice. The mutation result records previous_resource and resource in its lineage details. Crossover records selected_parent, either 0 or 1. Offspring receive fresh framework IDs even when their resource choice matches a parent's. An integer is immutable, so cloning can return the existing value safely. ResourceCodec encodes the integer directly and validates its range on restoration. A boolean is rejected as a resource ID despite Python's treatment of booleans as a kind of integer. Configuration and evaluation counts The application accepts one parameter under experiment.parameters: Parameter Default and supplied value Validation resource_quality [1.0, 1.8, 3.0, 5.0] A nonempty list of positive finite numbers; booleans are rejected The list's length determines the number of resources and the legal genotype indices. Unknown application parameters are errors. Resource quality does not evolve; it defines the environment in which resource choices evolve. The preset uses the following framework settings: Setting Value Evolutionary seed 24680 Population size 48 Parent selection Roulette using the current population's fitness values Survivor policy Complete replacement Mutation probability 0.75; otherwise choose crossover Maximum attempts per child 20 Termination Maximum generation 12 Generation 0 is the evaluated initial population. With no earlier stopping predicate, a complete preset run evaluates generations 0 through 12: 13 population-hook calls and 624 accepted individual evaluations. Parents are selected from the entire previous population, using its accepted scores. Every new generation is evaluated as a complete population so that scores reflect the new occupancy. For general settings, bounds, and available selection policies, see JSON configuration. Interpret history in its population context The run summary and inspect output distinguish the historical best record from the current population's statistics. Here, historical best means the largest individual share observed in its recorded population context. It does not mean that the winning resource index has a permanent fitness or will produce that score in a different population. Keep the associated generation and crowding metric when interpreting an observation. A historical-best candidate may have left the population, and the same resource can score differently in later generations. The analysis JSON preserves generation membership, individual observation histories, and genealogy so that these relationships can be examined together. Population mean fitness also needs care. Summing the shares of all occupants of one resource recovers that resource's quality. Consequently: mean_fitness = sum(qualities of occupied resources) / population_size When all four preset resources are occupied, the mean is approximately 10.8 / 48 = 0.225, regardless of how those 48 candidates are distributed among them. The allocation can change substantially while the mean stays constant. Inspect occupancy and individual shares alongside summary fitness. The example illustrates coupled evaluation; it does not establish a unique globally optimal allocation. Pause, continue, and change the experiment This preset has a fixed generation limit, making it convenient for seeing checkpoint continuation. Use a fresh directory for the paused run: uv run --locked python -m xevolution run --config examples/batch_problem.json --output runs/shared-resources-paused --pause-after-generation 2 uv run --locked python -m xevolution inspect runs/shared-resources-paused uv run --locked python -m xevolution resume runs/shared-resources-paused The pause commits generation 2 after three population evaluations, accounting for 144 accepted individual evaluations. Resume continues at the next transition and retains the configured generation-12 limit. It does not rescore the saved generation. Resuming an already terminated run leaves it terminated. Save changed qualities, population size, or evolutionary seed in a new configuration and start a new output directory. Resume reconstructs the saved configuration; editing the original JSON does not alter an existing run. Compatible code and explicit random/component state remain part of the checkpoint contract. The CLI reference describes graceful signals and recovery. To adapt the example to shared constraints, competition, or a coupled simulation, keep the population hook and replace its scoring model. If each candidate can be scored independently, the arithmetic and Digits examples show the simpler individual hook.","order":4},{"title":"Evolving a small neural network for Digits","url":"digits_demo.html","text":"Evolving a small neural network for Digits This example uses xevolution to choose the architecture of a small multilayer perceptron (MLP). Training learns each network's weights. Evolution changes its hidden layers and activation, trains the resulting candidate, and compares how many validation images it classifies correctly. The goal is an understandable example of the framework. Fitness is accuracy alone: the number of correct validation predictions divided by the number of validation images. A fitness of 0.925 means 92.5% correct. There is no loss, parameter-count penalty, or size preference hidden in this score. The package overview explains the shared evolution cycle. For simpler representations and the other evaluation hook, see the arithmetic and shared-resource guides, or start with the examples comparison. 1. Install and run Run these commands from the source project's root, beside pyproject.toml. Use Python 3.12 as the initial baseline. Every dependency is installed in the repository-local virtual environment created and managed by uv. uv python install 3.12 uv sync --locked --group dev --group neural uv run --locked --group neural python -m xevolution run --config examples/neural_digits_quick.json --output runs/digits-quick uv run --locked --group neural python -m examples.digits_report runs/digits-quick Keep UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT unset or pointing to this repository's .venv. Keep --group neural on commands that build, train, or restore this application. The neural group declares NumPy, scikit-learn, and threadpoolctl; uv records their resolved transitive dependencies in uv.lock. They are not dependencies of the installed xevolution core wheel. The Digits data ships with scikit-learn, so running the example needs no further dataset download. Once the uv environment is prepared, training needs neither a network connection nor a GPU. The run command prints a JSON summary containing best_fitness. The companion report displays the selected architecture and its accuracy as both counts and percentages, alongside a fixed one-hidden-layer, 32-neuron ReLU baseline. For the normal preset, use a fresh output directory: uv run --locked --group neural python -m xevolution run --config examples/neural_digits.json --output runs/digits uv run --locked --group neural python -m examples.digits_report runs/digits --output reports/digits.json --output on the report command is an optional JSON file outside the run directory. The command still prints its readable summary. The report validates the saved run and requires a completed evolutionary run before evaluating its test images. 2. What evolves? An individual carries an immutable architecture record. Its explicit JSON codec uses this representation: { \"hidden_layers\": [32, 16], \"activation\": \"relu\" } Each number is the number of individual neurons in that hidden layer. This example means 64 inputs, a hidden layer with 32 neurons, a hidden layer with 16 neurons, and ten output class scores. Every neuron in a layer receives all outputs from the preceding layer. A unit means one neuron, not a block of neurons. The allowed search space is intentionally small: Property Allowed values Number of hidden layers 1, 2, or 3 Neurons in each hidden layer 8, 16, 32, or 64 Hidden activation relu or tanh, shared across hidden layers Input size Fixed at 64 pixel values Output size Fixed at ten digit classes The largest architecture, 64 -> 64 -> 64 -> 64 -> 10, has 13,130 trainable weights and biases. The baseline, 64 -> 32 -> 10, has 2,410. Mutation resizes a layer, inserts a layer, removes a layer, or switches the activation. Inapplicable operations, such as removing the only hidden layer, use the framework's defined operator-inapplicability mechanism. Crossover combines parent descriptions within the same architecture bounds. Parents are never modified, and the framework assigns fresh child IDs and genealogy. The normal and quick presets use tournament parent selection with a tournament size of three and retain one elite individual. They select mutation with probability 0.7 and crossover with probability 0.3. Fitness ties follow the framework's stable policies; model size does not break ties. Retaining an elite preserves its architecture and identity. Its network is still trained afresh when the next population is evaluated, as required by xevolution's population-evaluation contract. 3. What happens during one evaluation? The application prepares a fixed stratified division of the 1,797 examples: Partition Examples Purpose Training 1,000 Learn weights using the selected architecture Validation 400 Compute the fitness used by evolution Final test 397 Evaluate the selected model after evolution finishes Pixel values are divided by their known maximum, 16. This transformation has no fitted statistics and uses no validation/test information. The split indices and data contents are fingerprinted for checkpoint compatibility. For each candidate, the evaluator: Creates a fresh scikit-learn MLPClassifier from the architecture. Initializes its weights with the recorded training_seed. Trains for exactly the configured number of epochs with Adam, fixed learning rate and regularization, and the same precomputed minibatch order. Predicts the validation labels and counts correct predictions. Returns accuracy as fitness, with numeric measurements describing the score and architecture. The initialization generator and the generator that produces epoch permutations are separate. Different architectures consume different numbers of random weights, so a shared initialization seed alone would not ensure the same ordering of training samples. Precomputed ordering keeps that part of the comparison fixed. Evolution uses its own managed random stream. The training loop makes one partial_fit call per epoch, preserving the candidate's Adam state between epochs. It does not reuse a trained network between candidate evaluations. The batch size is capped at the training partition's size for small test configurations. Numerical work uses one BLAS thread, and there is no candidate-level parallel executor or fitness cache. Training loss is used internally by the classifier to learn weights, as usual; it is not returned as fitness. Reported size and depth are descriptive metrics. Elapsed time is operational information and does not enter scientific history or evolutionary decisions. 4. Presets and configurable values Preset Population Generations evaluated Epochs per candidate Candidate evaluations Quick 6 0 through 3 10 24 Normal 8 0 through 5 30 48 Generation 0 is the initial evaluated population. With a fixed population size P and final generation G, evaluation count is P * (G + 1), including elites. The final report performs two additional model fits outside this count. Application parameters live under experiment.parameters in the JSON file: Parameter Default Meaning train_size 1000 Number of training images validation_size 400 Number of images used to calculate fitness test_size 397 Number of final held-out test images epochs 30 Complete passes through training data per candidate batch_size 128 Maximum examples in one training minibatch learning_rate 0.001 Fixed initial Adam learning rate alpha 0.0001 scikit-learn's L2 regularization coefficient split_seed 17 Seed used to create the fixed stratified split training_seed 23 Common model-initialization and derived shuffle seed Split sizes must add to 1,797 and must support all ten classes. Counts and seeds must be integers, excluding booleans. Learning rate and regularization must be finite and within their documented validation ranges; unknown parameter names are errors. Read DigitsSettings in the example for exact programmatic bounds. The top-level seed controls the evolutionary run. Changing it explores a different evolutionary trajectory while the explicit split/training seeds continue to define the same scoring task. Change a configuration only when starting a new run; resume uses the saved configuration. 5. Read the results uv run --locked --group neural python -m xevolution inspect runs/digits uv run --locked --group neural python -m xevolution analyze runs/digits --output reports/digits-history uv run --locked --group neural python -m examples.digits_report runs/digits --output reports/digits.json inspect reads checkpoint metadata. analyze exports the existing scientific history without training or evaluating anything. digits_report is a separate application-level final evaluation: it trains the recorded historical-best architecture afresh and verifies that its validation accuracy reproduces the saved score. It also trains the fixed 32-neuron ReLU baseline with the same training split and budget. Both architectures are fixed before final test prediction. The report computes each model's test accuracy once, with no selection based on those test scores. It does not train on the combined training-plus-validation set. This makes the reported comparison match the training protocol that evolution optimized. The report never advances the run, changes the committed journal, or appends its new evaluations to scientific history. Repeating the report in a compatible environment reproduces its results; it does retrain the two models. Optional report output must be outside the run directory. A run need not beat the fixed baseline to demonstrate the framework correctly. With only 400 validation images, one changed prediction moves accuracy by 0.25 percentage points. Short training budgets also favor architectures that learn well within that budget; they do not establish which architecture would win after convergence. Treat small differences as an illustration, not evidence of a generally superior classifier. 6. Pause and resume uv run --locked --group neural python -m xevolution run --config examples/neural_digits.json --output runs/digits-paused --pause-after-generation 2 uv run --locked --group neural python -m xevolution resume runs/digits-paused The engine checkpoints every completed generation, including generation 0. Restore reconstructs and validates the data, split, recipe, and numerical environment; restores explicit evaluator state; and resumes the saved evolutionary random streams. It does not train the saved generation again. The evaluator counts completed model fits through the explicit component-state contract. Prepared arrays are reconstructed from the bundled dataset and recorded settings, with integrity fingerprints. Trained estimators and their optimizer states are temporary evaluation work and are not placed in JSON checkpoints. If a crash interrupts a candidate fit or an uncommitted generation, that work can run again from the saved checkpoint. Fixed seeds and compatible numerical execution reproduce the same accepted scores. The guarantee requires matching application code, data, Python/library versions, and relevant numerical backend settings. It does not promise identical trajectories across different library versions, operating systems, or processor numerical implementations. The first Ctrl-C requests a pause after the current generation completes. Ordinary resume honors a saved terminal decision. Changing a generation limit in the original JSON file does not extend an already completed run. 7. Runtime and verification The architecture and data bounds are intended to keep CPU evaluation short. Actual measurements for this release are in verification.md. They are measurements of the stated execution environment, not a guarantee for every laptop. Time the full evaluation, including model construction and validation, when calibrating a different machine or training recipe. There is no wall-clock timeout that assigns a different fitness to a slow candidate. Set a small, fixed epoch budget before a run and leave margin below your time target. At five seconds per candidate the normal preset would take about four minutes for its 48 evaluations; at 30 seconds it would take about 24 minutes, before the final report. Run the optional behavioral and continuation tests with: uv run --locked --group dev --group neural python -m pytest -m neural The tests exercise real small MLP fits, accuracy accounting, immutable architectures, variation bounds, strict settings, checkpoint compatibility, fresh-process continuation, and final reporting. The core test suite can still run with the development group alone. Source references scikit-learn Digits loader and dataset description. UCI Optical Recognition of Handwritten Digits dataset, the source of scikit-learn's bundled subset. MLPClassifier parameters and incremental training. scikit-learn parallelism and numerical thread control. uv dependency groups.","order":5},{"title":"JSON configuration","url":"configuration.html","text":"JSON configuration Configuration selects application behavior and supplies its parameters. The framework validates core fields, and the application factory validates its own namespace. Arbitrary Python object graphs are not represented declaratively. Envelope { \"schema_version\": 1, \"seed\": 12345, \"experiment\": { \"factory\": \"examples.arithmetic:build_experiment\", \"parameters\": {\"target\": 64, \"terms\": 10, \"mutation_rate\": 0.01} }, \"evolution\": { \"population_size\": 100, \"mutation_probability\": 0.5, \"parent_selection\": {\"name\": \"roulette\"}, \"survival\": {\"name\": \"replace_all\"}, \"max_attempts_per_child\": 100 }, \"termination\": { \"any\": [ {\"max_generations\": 200}, {\"fitness_at_least\": 1.0} ] } } experiment.factory is an explicit package.module:factory reference or a name registered by a programmatic application. The CLI requires a resolvable factory. Programmatic Engine.create() can receive an already constructed Experiment and a RunConfig whose factory is absent. Defaults and policies Setting Default Validation and meaning schema_version 1 Only the supported integer version is accepted seed 0 Integer; booleans are rejected population_size 100 Positive integer; resulting completed population size mutation_probability 0.5 Finite value in [0, 1]; mutation or crossover route per child max_attempts_per_child 100 Positive integer; finite budget after operator/fallback exhaustion parent_selection {\"name\":\"roulette\"} Full previous population is eligible survival {\"name\":\"replace_all\"} Existing individuals retained before producing children Supported parent selectors: {\"name\": \"roulette\"} uses fitness directly as nonnegative weight. All-zero weights select uniformly; negative weights raise an error. {\"name\": \"uniform\"} samples members uniformly. {\"name\": \"tournament\", \"size\": 3} compares finite signed fitness among a positive number of sampled candidates. Size defaults to 3. Supported survivor selectors: {\"name\": \"replace_all\"} retains none. {\"name\": \"elitism\", \"count\": 1} retains the best eligible candidates, up to the configured count and target population size. Count defaults to 1 and must be nonnegative. elitist is also accepted as a policy-name alias. Ties use stable individual IDs. Parent sampling permits replacement and self-crossover. A custom selection policy is supplied through Experiment. Stopping Each stopping object contains exactly one predicate or combinator: Object Meaning {\"max_generations\": N} Stop at completed generation N; generation 0 is initialization {\"max_evaluations\": N} Stop after a complete generation reaches the accepted-result threshold {\"fitness_at_least\": F} Stop when current population fitness reaches finite threshold F {\"stagnation\": N} Stop after N transitions without a strictly better historical fitness {\"any\": [A, B]} Stop when any child condition stops {\"all\": [A, B]} Stop when all child conditions stop {\"not\": A} Negate one child condition Generation and evaluation limits are nonnegative integers; a stagnation window is positive. any and all require nonempty arrays. Composite predicates evaluate each child once per completed generation rather than short-circuiting away stateful updates. The evaluation threshold is a boundary check, not a hard mid-batch budget. The last population may cross it. An experiment must provide a stopping policy either here or as Experiment.stop; an empty declaration does not silently create an endless run. Ordinary resume preserves the saved policy and terminal decision. The CLI's --pause-after-generation option is operational and does not change these predicates. Seeded initial sampling Optional sampling maps simple paths to sampling specifications: { \"sampling\": { \"parameters.mutation_rate\": {\"kind\": \"log_uniform\", \"low\": 0.001, \"high\": 0.1}, \"evolution.population_size\": {\"kind\": \"integer\", \"low\": 40, \"high\": 80} } } Add this field to the complete envelope. Supported kinds are uniform (default), integer with inclusive integer bounds, and log_uniform with positive bounds. Paths are parameters.NAME or evolution.NAME; arbitrary nested traversal is not supported. Sampling uses a managed stream and sorted path order before component construction. Resolved values are validated, recorded, and restored. Resume does not resample them. Bounds and explicit changes Optional parameter_rules declares lifetime rules for core evolution settings: { \"parameter_rules\": { \"population_size\": {\"minimum\": 20, \"maximum\": 200, \"mutable\": true}, \"mutation_probability\": {\"minimum\": 0.0, \"maximum\": 1.0, \"mutable\": false} } } Rules apply to starting settings and registered adaptive updates. Invalid values are rejected rather than clamped. A hook returns, for example, state.effective_settings.updated(population_size=120); that schedules the next population and preserves the completed generation's settings snapshot. Applications can use ParameterRule and RangeParameter from xevolution.config for their own bounded numeric state. Register a future-affecting mutable RangeParameter in Experiment.components; its value and rule are explicit checkpoint state. Strict boundaries Unknown core fields, duplicate JSON object keys, unsupported versions, invalid types, booleans masquerading as numeric settings, and non-finite numbers are errors. Overflowing input such as 1e999 is rejected along with nonstandard NaN/Infinity tokens. Programmatic settings receive equivalent validation. Run directories record resolved starting configuration in config.json. Checkpoints retain both current-generation settings and the next effective settings. Loading configuration itself does not import or execute the factory; experiment construction is explicit.","order":6},{"title":"Command-line reference","url":"cli.html","text":"Command-line reference The xevolution console entry point and python -m xevolution expose the same commands. From a source checkout, use uv run --locked python -m xevolution so the repository's example factories are importable. All commands below run from the source project root, beside pyproject.toml. The examples overview links to complete guides for arithmetic, shared resources, and Digits. For a Digits run or resume, retain --group neural so uv includes its optional numerical dependencies: uv run --locked --group neural python -m xevolution run --config examples/neural_digits.json --output runs/digits uv run --locked --group neural python -m xevolution resume runs/digits The separate final-evaluation command is uv run --locked --group neural python -m examples.digits_report runs/digits. It trains two models and scores test data; the core analyze command below only projects existing history. See the Digits guide before using that report. When switching among example commands, keep --group neural or use --all-groups if you want the optional stack to remain installed. Create a fresh run uv run --locked python -m xevolution run --config examples/arithmetic.json --output runs/arithmetic The output directory must be new or empty. A populated directory is an error, not an instruction to resume. Configuration is validated, the application factory is resolved, generation 0 is initialized and evaluated, and subsequent generations execute until stopped or paused. Pause and resume uv run --locked python -m xevolution run --config examples/batch_problem.json --output runs/paused --pause-after-generation 2 uv run --locked python -m xevolution resume runs/paused --pause-after-generation N is an operational limit for this invocation. It does not change the recorded evolutionary stopping policy. A limit of 0 commits the evaluated initial population and returns. On resume a limit at or below the saved generation returns without another transition. The first Ctrl-C (SIGINT) or SIGTERM requests a graceful pause after the current generation completes and commits. It does not cancel an application evaluation already in progress. A second signal interrupts immediately; the next resume validates the last published checkpoint. Applications with long evaluations decide whether to provide their own cancellation or partial-work mechanism. Resume reads only the explicit checkpoint.json, restores recorded settings, components, random states, and observations, and repairs only an uncommitted journal tail. Saved-generation evaluation and stopping are not repeated. If a configured stopping predicate already terminated the run, ordinary resume leaves it terminated. Changing saved policies is not part of these commands. Successful run and resume print one JSON summary containing run identity, generation, phase, population size, accepted-evaluation count, historical best ID/fitness, and stopping reason. Inspect committed state uv run --locked python -m xevolution inspect runs/arithmetic Inspection validates the checkpoint and its committed data, then prints JSON settings, progress, statistics, best record, stopping state, and compatibility metadata. It does not import the application factory, evolve candidates, or truncate an uncommitted history tail. Large genotypes may appear as codec payloads or attachment references in the best record. Build analysis exports uv run --locked python -m xevolution analyze runs/arithmetic --output runs/arithmetic/reports The output defaults to RUN_DIR/reports when --output is omitted. The command replaces analysis.json, a deterministic projection containing generation summaries/settings/stopping, individuals with recorded births and observations, and parent-to-child genealogy edges. Ancestors remain available after leaving the population. Only the checkpoint's committed journal prefix contributes. Repeated analysis over the same prefix produces the same logical report and does not append scientific records, invoke evaluators, or consume evolutionary randomness. Reports are rebuildable outputs; checkpoint durability does not depend on them. Run directory Path Purpose config.json Resolved starting configuration checkpoint.json Latest authoritative state and committed journal reference history.jsonl Structured scientific records; an uncommitted tail may exist after a failure artifacts/ Immutable codec payload attachments, when used reports/analysis.json Rebuildable default analysis export Storage may also use writer-lock or temporary files for coordination. These do not replace the active checkpoint as the source of truth. Diagnostics uv run --locked python -m xevolution --help uv run --locked python -m xevolution --version uv run --locked python -m xevolution --verbose inspect runs/arithmetic --verbose is a global option, placed before the subcommand. Structured results go to standard output; command errors and diagnostic logging go to standard error. The CLI returns 0 on success, 1 for handled run/configuration/storage errors, 2 for argument-parsing errors, and 130 for an immediate interrupt. Unexpected application programming exceptions remain failures rather than being treated as operator inapplicability. See checkpointing for the exact saved boundary, recovery, compatibility, and external-side-effect limits.","order":7},{"title":"Application extension contracts","url":"extensions.html","text":"Application extension contracts Applications provide ordinary functions or compatible objects. No framework base class is required. Core bookkeeping stays in the framework, and arbitrary genotype payloads stay under application control. Constructing an experiment A configured factory has this signature: def build_experiment(config: RunConfig, random: RunRandom) -> Experiment[G]: ... Use config.parameters for application settings and config.evolution for resolved core settings. Validate your parameter namespace, including unknown keys. The factory constructs collaborators without creating the initial population or performing evaluation; restoration also calls the factory. Experiment accepts these collaborators: Field Contract initialize (rng: Random) -> G; produce one initial genotype clone (genotype: G) -> G; preserve independent ownership where required evaluate (individual: Individual[G], context: EvaluationContext) -> EvaluationResult evaluate_population (population: Population[G], context: EvaluationContext) -> Mapping[int, EvaluationResult] or an iterable of (id, result) pairs mutations Tuple of named OperatorSpec entries with (genotype, rng) -> VariationResult[G] operations crossovers Tuple of named entries with (first, second, rng) -> VariationResult[G] operations select_parent (population, rng) -> individual_id select_survivors (population, target_size, rng) -> Sequence[individual_id] stop (finalized_state) -> StopDecision or bool adaptive_hooks Tuple of `(state) -> EvolutionSettings codec A PayloadCodec[G] with explicit identity, version, encode, and decode components Stable named additional stateful collaborators identity, version Application compatibility identity and version compatibility Explicit JSON fingerprints for application data or external library versions Absent selection and stopping collaborators are constructed from configuration. A stopping policy must be supplied by the experiment or configuration. The default JSON codec is suitable only for genuinely JSON-native payloads. Supply a custom codec for tuples, arrays, bytes, graphs, or application objects. Genotype and observation ownership The engine does not assume that a genotype supports equality, ordering, hashing, truth testing, length, or indexing. Individuals are identified by their explicit IDs. Application operators may of course use operations supported by their own representation. An operator must leave both parents unchanged and return an independent child, unless sharing explicitly immutable data is safe. The framework assigns the offspring ID, birth generation, parents, fitness validity, and evaluation count. Do not copy a parent's wrapper and carry those fields into a child. A frozen dataclass does not freeze nested payloads. Supply a clone that handles your representation correctly. Historical bests use it to retain independent genotype snapshots; metadata and measurements are preserved separately. For an immutable string or integer, clone(value): return value is valid. Optional decoding belongs naturally inside the evaluator. The arithmetic example composes a decode() function with scoring and does not need a dummy decoder object in the engine. Evaluation Return one finite scalar fitness and an optional mapping of named finite numeric metrics. Zero is a valid score. Signed scores are valid; choose a selector that supports them. Metadata and encoded payloads have separate JSON contracts and should not be placed into numeric metrics. If evaluate_population is supplied, it runs directly, even if evaluate is also present. Otherwise the framework adapts the individual hook. A population hook may score candidates jointly or compare them in a shared environment; it need not match independent scoring. The result set must contain each requested ID exactly once and no other IDs. Sequence results can arrive in arbitrary order; the coordinator checks coverage and duplicates and applies all accepted results in deterministic population order. It validates the complete batch before applying scores or incrementing counts. One accepted result counts as one fitness evaluation; report underlying simulations or training steps as supplementary metrics. An individual evaluation receives context.rng. A batch can derive stable job contexts with context.for_individual(id, replicate=0). These logical contexts are independent of worker completion order. Applications own their execution backend and must return with no work in flight at the checkpoint boundary. See the shared-resource example, whose score depends on the number of other candidates choosing the same resource. Its implementation is examples/batch_problem.py in the source project. The arithmetic and Digits guides illustrate the individual hook. Variation and selection Register operators with stable names: OperatorSpec(\"domain_mutation\", mutate, weight=2.0) OperatorSpec(\"copy_fallback\", CloneMutation(clone), fallback=True) Import these helpers from xevolution.operators. Weighted entries are tried without replacement within an attempt; weight zero disables weighted selection. Fallbacks are an explicit separate designation and run in registration order after weighted choices, including an explicitly declared fallback whose weight is zero. A new attempt gets a fresh pool and new parent choices. The configured per-child budget bounds retries. Raise xevolution.errors.OperatorInapplicable only when an operation cannot apply to the selected parent(s). Unexpected exceptions remain failures, not signals to silently try another operation. Return operation details in VariationResult.details; the framework records stable parent and operator identities. OperatorSpec.weight is a validated mutable value and is checkpointed. Changes that affect future behavior must occur in registered adaptive/stateful logic, not observation-only reporting callbacks. Custom selectors return framework IDs from the supplied population. Parent selection sees the full previous population. Survivor selection independently chooses retained individuals, whose fitness will be reevaluated in the resulting population. Stateful components and adaptation Any collaborator retaining future-affecting information implements: def state_dict(self) -> JSONValue: ... def load_state_dict(self, state: JSONValue) -> None: ... State export is observational and returns an explicit JSON-native description. Restoration validates its schema and contents. Core collaborators exposing this contract are tracked automatically; use Experiment.components for additional shared state such as an evaluator environment, curriculum, or bounded parameter. Keep registration keys stable. Stateless functions may close over immutable configuration values, but mutable closure cells and global state must not hide future-affecting information. Frozen dataclasses are suitable for immutable component configuration, as the examples demonstrate. Their nested fields must also satisfy the configuration or registered-component contracts; a frozen wrapper does not make a captured list immutable. A Stateful object reached only through a function capture must be registered in Experiment.components, even when it exposes the state methods. Direct collaborators are registered automatically, and bound methods sharing one registered owner are restored only once. A stateful component may additionally implement checkpoint_identity() -> JSONValue to report immutable configuration that must match during restoration, such as an algorithm revision or a dataset digest. This optional identity is compared before loading mutable state; it does not replace state_dict(). Identity and state export must be observational and return strict JSON values. An adaptive hook receives finalized observations and may return new validated settings for the next transition. It may update its own explicitly saved state. It must not mutate the finalized population or its observations. The checkpoint records both generation_settings and the next effective_settings. Stopping runs once after these updates for each completed generation. Stateful custom predicates must save their windows and counters. Restoration honors the recorded decision instead of invoking the predicate again. Codecs and attachments A payload codec defines codec_id: str, version: int, and: def encode(self, value: G, writer: AttachmentWriter | None = None) -> JSONValue: ... def decode(self, value: JSONValue, reader: AttachmentReader | None = None) -> G: ... Encode required semantics explicitly. The built-in JsonCodec rejects lossy coercions; it is not a generic object-graph serializer. Large or binary payloads can use writer.put(bytes, media_type=...) and reader.get(reference). Codecs must reject a missing attachment context when they require one. Storage owns attachment integrity, ordering, and durability. Codec calls and component state export must not consume evolutionary randomness or mutate application state. For configured factories, restoration first reinstalls the saved pre-construction random snapshot and calls the factory with resolved parameters. Reconstructed components then receive their explicit state, payloads are decoded, and the completed-generation managed random states are installed last. Keep references to the supplied managed generators rather than replacing them during restoration. For application changes that alter state interpretation or future behavior, update compatibility identities/versions deliberately. See checkpointing for the full contract.","order":8},{"title":"Architecture","url":"architecture.html","text":"Architecture xevolution is a generic, headless evolutionary-computation library. Application code supplies the representation and problem; the framework coordinates its evolution, identities, measurements, history, and continuation. Module ownership All library modules live under src/xevolution/. Module Owns Main interactions models.py Shared individuals, populations, results, run state, and generation records Data exchanged by policies, the engine, tracking, and serialization protocols.py Structural contracts for application components Applications implement functions or compatible objects without inheriting framework classes errors.py Focused configuration, evaluation, reproduction, and checkpoint errors Components distinguish operator inapplicability from unexpected failures config.py Strict JSON input, validated settings, sampling, and explicit updates Supplies construction settings and separate completed/next-generation settings randomness.py Managed random streams, evaluation contexts, and stable work identities Initialization, variation, selection, evaluation, and state restoration evaluation.py Hook routing, complete result validation, deterministic application, and accepted counts Engine supplies the population; applications return observations selection.py Distinct parent and survivor policies Reproduction chooses parents; population assembly retains survivors operators.py Weighted operations, fallbacks, and cloning operations Supplies variation behavior and provenance to reproduction reproduction.py Finite offspring attempts and framework birth bookkeeping Reads the complete previous population and produces independent children termination.py Stopping predicates and logical composition Reads finalized state once per generation; exposes persistent predicate state engine.py Fresh initialization, transitions, adaptive updates, stopping, and commits Coordinates the policy modules and an optional store experiment.py Explicit composition of an application's collaborators Connects initialization, clone/codec, evaluator, operators, and policies registry.py Named and explicit Python factory resolution Reconstructs application behavior from recorded identities serialization.py Versioned conversion of typed state and application payloads Engine prepares state for storage and decodes restored records storage.py Files, attachments, journal offsets, atomic publication, and recovery Commits prepared state and exposes only committed scientific history tracking.py Statistics, independent historical snapshots, genealogy, and report projections Consumes finalized observations; supplies historical data to stopping cli.py Command parsing, operational logging, and user-facing process errors Calls public experiment/engine/storage operations __main__.py python -m xevolution entry point Delegates to the same CLI as the console script __init__.py Small public import surface Does not import examples or perform run-time work on import Shared models and protocols do not depend on the concrete engine or storage. Application behavior enters through composition. There is no miscellaneous utility module or runtime dependency on the old Xnet packages. Data ownership The framework owns individual IDs, population order, generation numbers, accepted-evaluation counts, provenance, and the commit boundary. An application owns genotype semantics, cloning, decoding, domain evaluation, and any private state retained by its components. IDs determine individual identity. The engine does not compare, hash, index, measure, or truth-test arbitrary genotype payloads. A frozen wrapper does not make a nested list, array, or graph immutable: operators must leave parents unchanged, and retained historical snapshots use the application's cloning contract. Numeric metrics supplement one scalar fitness. They do not create multiple objectives. Population summaries include each metric's contributing count; missing observations remain missing. Completed-generation lifecycle Fresh execution resolves settings, constructs components, creates genotypes, assigns IDs, and evaluates generation 0. A later transition: Captures the effective settings to use for the new generation. Selects survivors and determines how many children are needed. Selects parents from the complete previous population. Produces children through mutation or crossover with finite retry budgets. Assembles the new population and evaluates all members, including survivors. Validates all result identities and scores before applying observations. Updates statistics, independent historical snapshots, and registered adaptive components. Evaluates stopping once and retains the decision and predicate state. Commits the generation if persistence is enabled, then exposes its record. The population hook takes precedence when supplied. Otherwise the coordinator adapts the individual hook. A population hook can calculate jointly dependent fitness; the engine does not replace it with an independent loop. An adaptive update changes effective_settings for the next transition. The completed population retains its own generation_settings snapshot, so changing the next target population size cannot rewrite what just happened. In-memory execution follows the same lifecycle without opening a run directory. Failure inside a transition may have mutated application state; the library does not promise general in-memory rollback. Durable recovery starts from the last committed generation. Scientific records and operational messages Scientific history records generation membership, evaluation observations, statistics, births, parent links, operations, settings, and stopping decisions. Its committed prefix is the authority for reproducible analysis. Genealogy child indexes are derived from recorded parent links. Logging is for progress and diagnostics. It is not parsed to reconstruct scientific results, and its timing may differ after recovery. The CLI configures logging; importing or embedding the library does not. See extensions for application contracts, configuration for settings, and checkpointing for durability and restoration.","order":9},{"title":"Checkpoints and continuation","url":"checkpointing.html","text":"Checkpoints and continuation A checkpoint restores the same logical evolutionary run when application code, input data, component state, and numerical execution satisfy the documented contracts. Saving only an initial seed or a list of genotypes is insufficient. Saved boundary Persistent runs checkpoint generation 0 and every completed generation. The saved boundary follows complete evaluation, validation and application of all results, scientific tracking, adaptive updates, and one stopping decision. No evaluation jobs remain in flight. The next action is either a transition to the next generation or no further evolution because the run is terminated. Restoration does not rerun initialization, saved-generation evaluation, adaptive updates, or a stateful stopping predicate. A saved terminal decision remains terminal under ordinary resume. An operational pause requests a stop at the boundary and leaves a ready checkpoint unless an evolutionary predicate has independently terminated the run. Required state Checkpoints preserve ordered individuals and genotype payloads; IDs, births, and parentage; fitness and other observations; allocation and evaluation counters; completed-generation and next-transition settings; historical bests and improvement information; stopping decisions; component identities and state; all managed random states; codec and schema versions; and the identity, integrity, and byte length of the committed journal prefix. The evolution and evaluation random streams are separate. Stable evaluation contexts derive work identities from logical generation/individual/round/ replicate information, not worker order, process IDs, wall time, Python's randomized hash, or a fresh run identifier. Factory-based runs also save the original managed random state immediately after initial parameter sampling and before factory construction, under compatibility.construction_random. Resume installs that snapshot before reconstructing the factory with resolved settings, so random choices made while constructing collaborators can be reproduced. After component-state restoration and genotype decoding, the saved completed-generation random state is installed last. Initialization and evaluation still do not repeat. A programmatic run without a recorded factory requires an explicitly supplied, compatible Experiment when resumed. Stateful application objects must export and restore every value affecting future results. This includes adaptive probabilities, stopping windows, application generators, simulator or optimizer state, and meaningful caches. Stateless functions need no payload, but hidden mutable globals or closures do not become checkpointable automatically. Serialization Framework and application records use explicit codecs and versioned JSON. Required genotype semantics must survive encode/decode. Encoding and state export are observational: they must not mutate the run, advance its random generators, or evaluate candidates. JSON object keys are strings. Strict boundaries reject duplicate keys, unsupported versions, invalid types, and non-finite numbers, including values that overflow during parsing. Booleans are not numeric settings or fitness values. Tuple-based random state is reconstructed deliberately rather than passed back as nested JSON lists. The payload contract allows immutable attachments so application data need not always expand into JSON arrays. Attachment references carry format/version and integrity information. Referenced attachments must be durable before their checkpoint becomes authoritative. Storage writes attachment bytes to artifacts/\u003csha256>.bin. It reserves the JSON object key __xevolution_attachment__ for references containing version, SHA-256 digest, byte size, and media type. Use writer.put() and reader.get() instead of constructing these references or writing files from a codec. Commit order and visibility One coordinator writes a run. Workers return results rather than write history or checkpoints themselves. A commit proceeds in this order: Finalize state and prepare independent scientific records and payloads. Make required immutable attachments durable. Append scientific records to history.jsonl, flush, and synchronize the file. Write and synchronize a temporary checkpoint in the run directory. It names the committed history byte offset and integrity information. Atomically replace checkpoint.json and synchronize the directory entries required by the platform's local-file durability protocol. Report success; any subsequent reports read committed records. Atomic replacement establishes visibility to readers in the running system. Power-loss durability additionally depends on successful file and directory synchronization and the filesystem's guarantees. Failure before directory synchronization may leave an older or newer checkpoint after a machine failure; recovery validates whichever checkpoint survived. Generation 0 has no preceding checkpoint to recover if its initial publication did not survive. These are local-file guarantees. A remote or unusual filesystem can have weaker semantics. A failure to save is reported as a failure to make the new generation durable, not as a successful evolutionary commit. The implementation synchronizes directory entries on POSIX systems. On other platforms it supplies atomic checkpoint publication and process-crash recovery, but does not claim power-loss durability for directory changes. A writable session holds the run's writer lock until close(); use engine/store context managers to release it reliably. Recovery and journal tails The active checkpoint determines the authoritative journal prefix. An abrupt failure may leave extra bytes in history.jsonl after that prefix. Read-only inspection and analysis ignore them. A resumed writer truncates or resets the journal to the saved committed offset before appending new records. Missing or corrupt committed history or attachments is an error. Recovery does not invent replacement observations or reevaluate candidates from an already committed generation. Temporary checkpoint files and arbitrary recently modified files do not supersede checkpoint.json. A portable run is a bundle: its checkpoint, required journal prefix, and any referenced attachments, together with compatible application code and data. Copying a checkpoint manifest alone does not necessarily create a resumable run. Application and environment limits Work in an interrupted, uncommitted generation can repeat. External calls needing deduplication must use application-level idempotency or result lookup. Continuation inside an arbitrary long evaluation is not supplied by the framework. A training evaluator may need model, optimizer, scheduler, data-position, and application random state of its own. Changing external data or using nondeterministic numerical operations can change future results even when engine state restores exactly. Supporting multiple Python versions is not a promise of identical trajectories or checkpoint portability between those versions. Operational messages and their timestamps need not match across executions. Compatibility checks must succeed before a resumed run claims exact continuation. Keep the application's factory, codec identifiers/versions, and registered state keys stable; use a new compatibility version when semantics change. The current checks compare framework source fingerprints, package and schema versions, Python implementation/version and platform information, application identity/version and explicit compatibility entries, codec identity/version, and the registered component composition. A source edit or a Python patch upgrade can therefore require the original environment to resume a saved run. These checks are deliberately strict; inspect and analyze can still read committed records without loading the application factory.","order":10},{"title":"Porting notes","url":"porting_notes.html","text":"Porting notes The source baseline is the generic Java xevolution subproject at commit 509c673e502f0c27daa6b91f39cc298404b59ae2. The implementation follows the repository's port specification. Scope and mapping Java responsibility Python owner Genome, population, attributes, and identity models.py, with explicit IDs, typed core fields, and named metrics Genetics factories and genome decoders Application initialization, cloning, codecs, and optional decoding composed in experiment.py Evolution strategy engine.py Evaluation strategies evaluation.py and application evaluation hooks Parent selection and survival filters Separate policies in selection.py Mutation, crossover, and operator selection operators.py and reproduction.py Configuration, ranges, and stopping conditions config.py and termination.py XML persistence Explicit versioned representations in serialization.py and durable file operations in storage.py Population statistics and genealogy tools tracking.py and the inspect/analyze commands Logging and command-line option wrappers Python logging and argparse Needed xutil behavior is incorporated where it belongs. Collection builders, getter/setter wrappers, lazy-construction holders, and Java interface/abstract/ implementation hierarchies are not reproduced as parallel Python hierarchies. NEAT, simulation implementations, batch-simulation infrastructure, all graphical interfaces, screenshot tools, and the unfinished operator workbench are excluded. Application evaluators may use their own simulators, numerical libraries, or execution backends. The framework itself has no such runtime dependencies. Intended algorithmic behavior retained Generation 0 is the evaluated initial population. Parent selection and survivor selection are separate. The complete previous population remains eligible for parent selection, including nonsurvivors. Complete replacement is the default. Every member of the resulting population, including a retained survivor, is evaluated each generation. The mutation probability chooses mutation or crossover for one offspring. It does not schedule a second mutation stage after crossover. The objective is one finite scalar fitness, maximized by default. Additional named measurements support analysis without becoming extra objectives. Whole-population evaluation is a first-class application hook. It may depend jointly on all candidates rather than reproduce a loop of independent scores. The Java generation lifecycle and the application batch-evaluation example establish these extension and lifecycle boundaries. Deliberate corrections and changes Explicit JSON state replaces Java serialization. Configuration, checkpoints, and scientific history have versioned representations. Application codecs preserve genotype semantics. Java files and Java random trajectories are not compatibility targets. Fresh creation and restoration are separate operations. The Java runner loads the most recently modified XML file in its output directory. Python resumes only the explicit authoritative checkpoint and does not initialize or reevaluate its saved population. Reproduction is bounded. Positive-weight operators are tried without replacement within an attempt, followed by explicit fallbacks. Each child has a finite attempt budget. Only the defined inapplicability condition is recoverable; programming failures propagate. Selection weights are validated. Direct roulette weights must be finite and nonnegative. All-zero weights select uniformly. Negative fitness is valid data but requires a compatible selector or an explicit weight transformation; it does not silently remove selection pressure. Saved counters allocate identities. Removed individuals cannot cause an ID to be reused after restoration. Genotype comparison and hashing do not determine identity. Newborn age is zero. Age is the current generation minus birth generation, rather than a separately mutable field. Missing measurements remain missing. Each metric's statistics include its own contributing count. Missing values are not replaced by zero or divided by the full population size. Historical observations are independent. Retained best individuals and scientific records cannot be rewritten by later mutation or reevaluation of live candidates. Equal fitness under a different ID is not an improvement. The framework owns accepted-evaluation accounting. Evaluators return results. The coordinator validates complete ID coverage before applying any scores or incrementing counts. Scientific history shares the checkpoint boundary. Only the committed journal prefix contributes to reports. A resumed writer discards an uncommitted tail before appending; missing or corrupt committed data is an error. The original reproduction loop, operator-selection reset behavior, weight normalization, and population statistics are the reference points for the corresponding corrections. Exact continuation is a contract for compatible application code, data, state, and numerical execution. It does not imply cross-version or cross-platform numerical equivalence, or exactly-once external side effects. See checkpointing.","order":11},{"title":"Verification report: xevolution 0.1.1","url":"verification.html","text":"Verification report: xevolution 0.1.1 This is the preserved Python release verification, recorded before the documentation was reorganized into Markdown and HTML editions. Its wheel, source, and lockfile hashes describe that tested release. New documentation build and link checks are recorded separately in the documentation build guide; they do not replace or rerun the release checks below. Verified on 2026-10-21 (UTC) using CPython 3.12.14 on Linux x86-64. This report records executed local checks for version 0.1.1. The accompanying verification.json contains the test counts, environment, wheel identity, source hashes, and complete Digits measurements. See the Digits walkthrough for installation and run instructions. Executed release checks All three test runs below completed with zero failures and zero errors. Test environment Passed Skipped Elapsed time Source checkout, development and neural dependencies 246 0 11.96 s Installed wheel, without numerical dependencies 196 2 modules 4.36 s Installed wheel, with neural dependencies 246 0 13.87 s The two collection skips are the optional neural example and reporting test modules. The core suite runs successfully without NumPy, scikit-learn, SciPy, or threadpoolctl. With the optional group installed, all 50 additional neural and reporting tests run. Ruff formatting passed for 47 Python files, and Ruff linting was clean. Strict mypy passed for 21 files: the core package and both neural example modules. uv build --offline produced the version 0.1.1 wheel and source distribution. Both complete Digits presets and their final reports ran through the public command-line interfaces; their measured results appear below. Interpreter scope and prior verification The current release was exercised locally on CPython 3.12.14 only. Version 0.1.0 had previously passed its then-complete 196-test suite on CPython 3.12.14, 3.13.16, 3.14.8, and the 3.15.0rc3 release candidate. Those historical checks predate this release's neural example and do not establish that the 0.1.1 suite has run on those other interpreters. The included reference GitHub Actions workflow configures core jobs for Python 3.12, 3.13, 3.14, and the named 3.15 release-candidate preview, plus neural jobs for Python 3.12, 3.13, and 3.14. Hosted CI was not executed for this delivery. macOS and Windows were not exercised locally. Support for several Python versions does not imply that checkpoints can be transferred between them with identical continuation. Exact installed-wheel verification The built wheel was installed into a disposable environment created with uv. The test working directory, outside the original checkout, contained copies of tests/, examples/, and pyproject.toml, with no src/ package. Tests ran through that environment's interpreter directly. They first ran without the optional numerical dependencies and then with the neural dependencies installed. Both runs exercised the installed core wheel. The checks confirmed: import xevolution resolved inside that environment's site-packages. The wheel contained py.typed and declared no core runtime dependencies. The console entry point and python -m xevolution passed both --help and --version. All 196 core tests passed without the numerical stack; all 246 tests passed once the neural dependencies were present. All 20 package files, including py.typed, were byte-identical between the delivered source package and the tested wheel. The tested wheel is xevolution-0.1.1-py3-none-any.whl, with SHA-256: f8f498f503f4742303ffde8c006c3352bd99812ccffdadc017244c1cfd8bd9a6 Example applications are included in the source distribution and project archive. They are not installed inside the core wheel; the copied examples above provide application factories for the installed-wheel tests. Digits CPU measurements Method and numerical environment Measurements used the Linux environment above, with glibc 2.39, NumPy 2.5.4, SciPy 1.18.1, scikit-learn 1.9.1, and threadpoolctl 3.7.0. The recorded OpenBLAS backends reported versions 0.3.31.dev and 0.3.34.106.0, both with the SkylakeX dispatch architecture. Each BLAS backend was limited to one thread during numerical work. This backend label is not a measurement of a particular laptop's processor performance. For each fixed architecture below, three fresh complete evaluator calls were timed sequentially with a monotonic performance counter. Data and epoch orders were prepared beforehand. Each call included model construction, training, validation prediction, accuracy calculation, and returned metrics. There was no warmup fit, fitted-model cache, or concurrent project test run. Imports, evaluator setup, and checkpoint I/O are outside these per-candidate timings; data preparation was timed separately at about 0.014 seconds. These calls used the normal recipe: 1,000 training images, 400 validation images, 30 epochs, batch size 128, learning rate 0.001, regularization 0.0001, split seed 17, and training seed 23. The architectures were fixed timing probes measured independently of the two evolutionary presets. Hidden layers and activation Parameters Median of 3 calls Largest observed call Validation accuracy on all 3 calls [32], ReLU 2,410 0.105 s 0.105 s 86.75% [64, 64, 64], ReLU 13,130 0.217 s 0.223 s 94.75% [64, 64, 64], tanh 13,130 0.210 s 0.235 s 95.00% The largest observed time is the largest of three measurements, not a runtime bound. These results describe this execution environment; they do not guarantee a time limit on another machine or under another workload. Complete preset runs Each preset was run once with its recorded evolutionary seed of 42. Search timing covered a fresh CLI process, including interpreter/library startup, data preparation, every candidate evaluation, and generation checkpoint I/O. The report was timed as a separate fresh process, including startup, its two new model fits, validation/test scoring, and JSON output. Dependency installation was outside both timers. Preset Evaluated generations Candidate fits Epochs per fit Search time Additional report time Quick 0 through 3 24 10 2.64 s 1.45 s Normal 0 through 5 48 30 7.95 s 1.38 s Generation 0 and reevaluated elites are included in the candidate-fit counts. The report's two fits are separate from evolutionary evaluation accounting. It reproduced each selected architecture's saved validation accuracy before scoring the selected model and fixed baseline on the 397 held-out test images. Preset and model Hidden layers and activation Validation correct / total Test correct / total Quick: selected [64, 64], tanh 354 / 400 (88.50%) 360 / 397 (90.68%) Quick: fixed baseline [32], ReLU 260 / 400 (65.00%) 267 / 397 (67.25%) Normal: selected [64, 32, 64], tanh 378 / 400 (94.50%) 385 / 397 (96.98%) Normal: fixed baseline [32], ReLU 347 / 400 (86.75%) 366 / 397 (92.19%) Each baseline used the same training partition and epoch budget as its preset. Test scores did not select the architectures. These are demonstration results from one fixed split and one evolutionary seed per preset, not an estimate of expected search quality across seeds or datasets. Short training budgets affect which architectures perform well; the measurements do not establish general architecture-search superiority. Complete unrounded timings, fingerprints, and report fields are retained under digits_demo in verification.json. Checkpoint and acceptance evidence tests/test_resume.py launches independent processes and compares uninterrupted execution with restoration at generations 0 and 3. It compares the complete logical checkpoint body, committed scientific history, and attachment bytes, excluding only operational run IDs. Processes use different PYTHONHASHSEED values. The application exercises opaque mutable genotypes, explicit binary codecs and cloning, shared stateful components, application RNG state, managed RNG Gaussian caches, sampled construction settings, adaptive operator weights, changing population sizes, elitism, and a stateful stopping window. A separate process exits abruptly during generation 2. Recovery restores generation 1, removes an injected uncommitted journal tail, and reaches the same final state and history. Other tests confirm that saved-generation evaluation, adaptive callbacks, and stopping are not repeated, and that terminal resume does not change scientific files. The neural suite adds real small MLP fits and fresh-process restoration from generations 0 and 1, comparing all logical state and scientific history. It checks deterministic repeated training, strict architecture/settings/state validation, parent independence, disjoint data splits, and a sentinel that fails if search accesses test arrays. Reporting tests verify the saved validation score, two fixed test evaluations, repeatable reports, and unchanged run files. Specification cases Executed evidence A01–A02: installation and evaluation hooks Exact-wheel checks, arithmetic and joint population evaluation, and CLI tests. A03–A04: population construction and ownership Parent/survivor separation, monotonic IDs, age, full reevaluation, and next-transition settings. A05–A06: corrected algorithms Signed/all-zero/extreme weights, explicit fallbacks, bounded retries, route validation, and programming-error propagation. A07: retained observations Independent best clones, immutable journal records, and per-metric contributing counts. A08–A09: restoration boundary New-process comparisons, saved callback/stopping state, and abrupt mid-generation exit. A10–A11: persistence and recovery Real files, writer exclusion, synchronization/replacement failure injection, tail truncation, and corrupt/missing history or attachments. A12: repeatable analysis Repeated analysis without duplicate generations, births, or genealogy edges. A13: complete explicit state RNG/configuration restoration, original factory construction state, shared-component alias topology, codec identity, and incompatibility rejection. A14: distinct CLI operations Fresh-output protection, explicit/terminal resume, inspection without application imports, analysis, and a real signal during generation-0 evaluation. Component tests also reject obvious hidden mutable or unregistered stateful captures, unmanaged RNG references, and payload decoding that changes restored component state. Exact continuation still depends on complete application state and compatible code, data, and numerical execution. It does not promise exactly-once external side effects for uncommitted work or numerical equality across platforms. See checkpointing.md and extensions.md for the application contracts. Tools and reproduction The verified development tools were uv 0.12.23, pytest 9.1.1, Ruff 0.17.1, and mypy 2.4.0. All Python dependencies were installed in environments managed by uv. To repeat the complete source checks from the project root: uv sync --locked --group dev --group neural uv run --locked --group dev --group neural ruff format --check . uv run --locked --group dev --group neural ruff check . uv run --locked --group dev --group neural mypy src/xevolution examples/neural_digits.py examples/digits_report.py uv run --locked --group dev --group neural python -m pytest uv build The executed build used uv build --offline after uv had populated its isolated build cache. Build requirements are declared in pyproject.toml; the lockfile records project and development dependency resolution. Isolated build dependencies are managed by uv and are not claimed to be fully pinned by uv.lock. Full preset and pause/resume commands are in digits_demo.md. The generic port follows the included specification and Java baseline commit 509c673e502f0c27daa6b91f39cc298404b59ae2. Source mappings, deliberate corrections, and retained licensing are described in porting_notes.md. NEAT, graphical/workbench components, and Java file/run compatibility remain outside the agreed port.","order":12},{"title":"xevolution Python Port: Architecture and Implementation Specification","url":"port_specification.html","text":"xevolution Python Port: Architecture and Implementation Specification This is a documentation copy of the repository-root xevolution_python_port_spec.md. It defines the agreed port contract and target design. For implemented behavior, start with the package overview, example guides, and release verification. Repository paths in the specification remain relative to the source project root. Version: 1.0 Date: October 20, 2026 Target: Python 3.12 and later Status: Specification based on the agreed project outline; implementation has not begun Java baseline: xtructure/xnet-public, commit 509c673e502f0c27daa6b91f39cc298404b59ae2 Contents Purpose and agreed scope Design principles Repository layout Python modules and interactions Data model and ownership Application extension contracts Generation lifecycle and algorithms JSON configuration Randomness and deterministic execution Checkpoint and recovery contract Tracking, genealogy, and reporting Command-line interface and run directories Java functionality and utility migration Examples and documentation Verification and acceptance criteria Implementation sequence Source references 1. Purpose and agreed scope Create a standalone, headless Python evolutionary-computation library that preserves the useful functionality of Java xevolution, corrects identified defects, and supports a broad range of application-defined representations and evaluators. The architecture must support experiments through Python code and a command-line interface. It must retain population statistics, historical results, and genealogy independently of any graphical interface. The Java repository separates the generic evolution framework from NEAT, simulation, and batch-simulation implementations. Its xevolution module declares xutil as its direct sibling dependency. This port follows that boundary and incorporates the required utility behavior into the new Python package. J2, J3 1.1 Agreed decisions Topic Required scope Evolution framework Port the generic xevolution functionality. NEAT Exclude the xneat implementation from this port. Preserve extension points for future applications. Graphical interface Exclude all GUI components, visualization callbacks, screenshot tools, and the unfinished operator workbench. Java interoperability No compatibility with Java configuration files, population files, serialized classes, or historical runs is required. Configuration Use versioned JSON configuration instead of XML. Application assumptions Support arbitrary genotype data and application-defined evaluation. Do not require a numerical-array, neural-network, or simulation representation. Evaluation Preserve both individual and whole-population hooks. Optimization Use one scalar objective, with higher fitness preferred. Keep parent and survivor selection extensible. Algorithm fidelity Preserve intended evolutionary behavior while correcting defects and making ambiguous policies explicit. Reproducing Java random trajectories is not required. Checkpoints Support continuation from a committed, completed generation, subject to explicit application-state and determinism contracts. Other Xnet utilities Reimplement necessary behavior inside xevolution, using Python facilities where appropriate. This specification does not require built-in distributed execution, GPU execution, a simulation engine, multiobjective optimization, or resumption inside an arbitrary external evaluation. Applications may provide such capabilities behind the evaluation contracts. 1.2 Requirement language Must identifies required behavior for the initial implementation. Should identifies a recommended implementation choice. May identifies an optional capability or a choice left to the implementer. Illustrative signatures describe contracts; they are not complete source-code implementations. The package name in this specification is xevolution. No distribution has been published or installed as part of preparing this document. 2. Design principles 2.1 Organize by responsibility Use a shallow package containing cohesive modules. Java interface, abstract-base-class, and implementation triples should generally become a small record, a function, or a typed protocol. Introduce a subpackage only when the implementation grows enough to justify it. Stateful objects remain appropriate for components that actually own state. The goal is clear ownership and composition, rather than eliminating classes. 2.2 Separate domain calculations from bookkeeping Application components create genotypes, calculate scores, choose candidates, and perform domain-specific variation. Framework code owns individual identities, evaluation accounting, population assembly, generation numbering, provenance, and durable commits. 2.3 Make data and execution independent The core must not require NumPy, PyTorch, a particular graph library, a simulator, or an executor. Applications can use these behind their hooks. A whole-population evaluator may perform jointly dependent calculations; it need not be equivalent to independent individual evaluations. 2.4 Keep persistence out of genetic objects Individuals, populations, selectors, and operators must not open checkpoint files or write reports directly. Serialization and storage own those operations. This permits in-memory use through scripts, notebooks, and tests without creating a run directory. 2.5 Make reproducibility an explicit contract Run-owned randomness, stable work identities, deterministic ordering, component state, and checkpoint boundaries must be designed together. A seed alone is insufficient to describe the current position of a running experiment. 2.6 Keep the initial runtime small The initial core should use the Python standard library. Use a project-local virtual environment managed with uv, and commit the dependency lockfile. uv provides the project environment and records resolved dependency versions in uv.lock. P1 Use type annotations and typing.Protocol for extension contracts. Protocols allow structural typing without requiring application classes to inherit from framework classes. Runtime validation of configuration, returned results, and saved state remains necessary; annotations do not enforce values during execution. P2 3. Repository layout Use an installable src/ layout. Keeping the import package beneath src/ helps tests exercise the installed distribution and exposes packaging mistakes that direct imports from a repository root can conceal. P3 All paths in this document are relative to the new repository root unless explicitly identified as Java source references. Path Purpose pyproject.toml Package metadata; Python requirement >=3.12; build configuration; development dependencies; the xevolution console entry point; test and code-quality configuration. uv.lock Resolved development dependency versions. README.md Installation, a minimal runnable experiment, and links to extension and checkpoint documentation. AGENTS.md Repository instructions for coding agents, including component ownership, randomness, checkpoint invariants, and behavioral verification. src/xevolution/ Installable library containing the modules in Section 4. src/xevolution/py.typed Package typing marker. examples/ Small application examples and their JSON configurations. tests/ Behavioral, integration, and recovery tests. tests/fixtures/ Small configuration, checkpoint, and application-payload fixtures. docs/markdown/ Package overview, three example guides, architecture, extension, configuration, checkpoint, CLI, porting, and verification documentation. docs/html/ Cross-linked HTML generated from the Markdown sources, with local reading assets. docs/build_docs.py Offline documentation builder and local link/fragment validation. .github/workflows/ci.yml Installation, tests, and code-quality checks on supported Python versions. The generated local environment and experiment output directories must be excluded from version control. The package must be tested on Python 3.12 and the newer Python versions it advertises as supported. Supporting several Python versions does not imply that an exact-resume checkpoint can move between them without compatibility checks. 4. Python modules and interactions All modules below live directly under src/xevolution/. There are 19 Python modules, including the two package entry-point modules. 4.1 Shared data and extension contracts Module Responsibility Interactions models.py Defines shared records such as Individual, Population, EvaluationResult, VariationResult, RunState, and immutable generation and lineage records. Provides the data exchanged by evaluation, reproduction, tracking, and persistence. Contains no file I/O or application-specific calculations. protocols.py Defines contracts for initialization, cloning, optional decoding, individual evaluation, population evaluation, parent selection, survival selection, mutation, crossover, termination, payload codecs, and stateful components. Allows applications to supply functions or compatible objects. Behavior modules depend on these contracts rather than concrete application implementations. config.py Loads and validates JSON configuration, applies defaults, and represents effective settings with typed records. Supports explicit bounds, optional seeded parameter sampling, and validated setting changes where needed. Supplies configuration to experiment construction and execution. Coordinates sampling with randomness.py; effective values are included in run state and checkpoints. randomness.py Owns per-run random generators, exports and restores their complete states, and derives reproducible evaluation contexts from stable work identities. Supplies randomness to initialization, selection, variation, and evaluation. Its persistent state is saved by storage. errors.py Defines focused exceptions for configuration errors, invalid evaluation results, inapplicable operators, exhausted reproduction attempts, and checkpoint failures or incompatibility. Lets reproduction distinguish expected operator inapplicability from programming failures; lets the CLI report errors without changing library behavior. 4.2 Evolutionary behavior Module Responsibility Interactions evaluation.py Adapts individual evaluation to population evaluation, invokes custom population hooks, validates returned results, and owns evaluation bookkeeping. Receives population data and evaluation context from the engine. Associates results with stable IDs and applies accepted scores and metrics in a defined order. selection.py Implements distinct parent-selection and survivor-selection contracts. Includes roulette, uniform, and tournament parent selection, complete replacement, and simple elitist survival. Reproduction uses parent selection; generation assembly uses survivor decisions. Selectors consume evaluated population data and explicit random context when needed. operators.py Represents mutation and crossover operations, weighted operator pools, explicit fallbacks, recoverable inapplicability, and structured operation provenance. Provides baseline cloning operations. Application operators produce genotype data and details. Reproduction selects and invokes them, then assigns offspring identity and lineage. reproduction.py Produces the required offspring using parent selection, mutation-or-crossover choice, operator selection, finite retry budgets, and offspring construction. Reads the complete previous population and survivor decisions. Uses selection, operators, and run-owned ID allocation; returns a complete offspring set or raises an error. termination.py Provides generation, evaluation-count, fitness-threshold, and stagnation predicates, plus logical composition and custom predicate support. Reads finalized run state. Any persistent counters or windows are exposed for checkpointing. engine.py Coordinates fresh initialization, one generation, repeated execution, registered adaptive updates, termination, and generation commits. Exposes incremental execution and a convenience run interface. Delegates to reproduction, evaluation, tracking, termination, and an optional store. Exposes completed generation records to callers. Parent selection and survivor selection remain different contracts even though they initially share a module. One chooses who reproduces; the other chooses which existing individuals remain. 4.3 Experiment construction, persistence, and tools Module Responsibility Interactions experiment.py Defines an Experiment composition holding application initialization, evaluation, variation, selection, stopping, cloning, and codec collaborators. Provides application-factory construction helpers. Connects validated settings with application code and produces the components used by the engine. registry.py Resolves configured short names or explicit package.module:factory references into experiment or component factories. Used for fresh construction and restoration. Resolves behavior from recorded identities and settings; it does not serialize live Python object graphs. serialization.py Converts framework records, random state, component state, and application payloads to versioned representations and restores them. Includes JSON-native codecs and a contract for other payload formats. Called by storage. Uses explicit application codecs, preserves required types and numerical precision, and reconstructs values such as tuple-based random state correctly. storage.py Owns run directories, checkpoint loading, atomic checkpoint replacement, the structured journal, attachment references, and committed-state recovery. Receives finalized state and generation records from the engine. Calls serialization and provides committed history to inspection and analysis. tracking.py Computes generation statistics, maintains independent historical-best snapshots, records births and provenance, queries genealogy, and builds analysis projections. Consumes finalized populations and variation records. Provides information used by stopping and persistence; reconstructs reports from committed history without evolution. cli.py Implements run, resume, inspect, and analyze; handles arguments, operational logging, and user-facing errors. Calls configuration, registry, experiment construction, engine, storage, and tracking. Interpreter exit is confined to the command boundary. __init__.py Exposes a small public API containing principal records and experiment/run/resume entry points. Provides convenient imports. Importing the package must not configure logging, instantiate an experiment, or consume randomness. __main__.py Delegates python -m xevolution to cli.main(). Shares the implementation of the installed xevolution command. 4.4 Dependency and ownership rules Shared records and protocols must not depend on concrete execution, storage, or CLI implementations. Application code is introduced through experiment construction; the core engine must not import example applications. serialization.py owns representation conversion. storage.py owns durability, journal positions, and file operations. tracking.py owns the interpretation of scientific records. These responsibilities must not be duplicated across modules. Operational logging uses the standard logging package. Scientific history uses structured generation records; it must not be recovered by parsing diagnostic log messages. 5. Data model and ownership 5.1 Principal records Use Individual[G] for the framework wrapper around arbitrary genotype data of type G. This corresponds to the role of Java Genome\u003cD>. A genotype may be a string, graph, array, tensor, or application-defined object. J4 Record Required meaning Individual[G] Stable individual ID; genotype; birth generation; parent IDs; latest fitness or unevaluated status; additional metrics; accepted-evaluation count; optional metadata. Population[G] Deterministically ordered individuals, lookup by ID, and optional population metadata. Enforces unique IDs. EvaluationResult One finite scalar fitness and named supplementary measurements. Underlying simulations, training steps, or other effort can be reported as additional measurements. VariationResult[G] A newly produced genotype and structured operation details. It does not allocate framework IDs or update the population. RunState[G] Current completed generation and phase; population; settings used for that generation and effective settings for the next transition; ID and evaluation counters; historical bests; improvement information; termination status; other framework state affecting continuation. GenerationRecord An immutable account of a finalized generation: population membership identifiers, measurements needed by reporting, births, operation provenance, and relevant state changes. Small additional context or decision records, such as EvaluationContext and StopDecision, may be added to models.py when they clarify a public contract. They do not require separate modules. 5.2 Identity, age, and evaluation status Individual IDs must be unique within a run. Cross-run references use run identity together with individual ID. Allocate individual IDs from an explicit monotonic run-state counter. Never reconstruct the counter from current survivors. Genotype equality must not determine individual uniqueness or population membership. An unevaluated individual has fitness=None, or an equivalently explicit unevaluated state. Zero is a valid fitness. Generation 0 is the initial evaluated population. An individual born in generation g has age zero in that generation; age is derived as current generation minus birth generation. Offspring receive fresh identity and evaluation bookkeeping. Copying a parent must not copy its evaluation count, fitness validity, or unrelated transient attributes into the new individual. 5.3 Mutable genotype data Freezing the wrapper does not freeze a nested genotype. Operators must leave parent data unchanged and return an independent child, unless sharing immutable data is explicitly valid. Applications must supply a cloning operation when their representation needs one. The framework must not assume that a shallow copy is sufficient, nor require a universal deep copy of every large payload. Historical best records and retained scientific observations must be independent of mutable live individuals. Use the application clone/codec contract to preserve the required genotype snapshot, and copy associated metadata. A later fitness update must not change an earlier generation's recorded result. 5.4 Metrics and metadata Keep the primary scalar objective separate from supplementary numeric metrics and nonnumeric metadata. Complexity is an optional application-provided measurement; the framework must not infer it from the representation. Every individual must have valid fitness before it participates in selection. Supplementary metrics may be absent. Statistics must report the number of contributing observations and must not turn missing values into zero. If no observations exist, represent the statistic as absent or null, consistently. 6. Application extension contracts 6.1 Required separation of responsibilities Extension Application responsibility Framework responsibility Initialization Produce initial genotype data from parameters and explicit random context. Assign IDs, create individual records, and validate the initial population. Cloning Copy genotype data according to its representation and ownership rules. Use cloning where offspring or retained snapshots require independence. Decoding Optionally convert genotype data to an evaluable phenotype. Permit composition inside evaluation without requiring a dummy decoder. Individual evaluation Return fitness and measurements for one individual. Validate the result and maintain accepted-evaluation bookkeeping. Population evaluation Evaluate the requested population jointly or in batches, returning results associated with requested IDs. Validate coverage and identities and apply accepted results deterministically. Parent selection Identify parents from the supplied evaluated population. Validate selected IDs and invoke variation. Survivor selection Identify existing individuals that remain. Validate survivors and assemble the next population. Mutation/crossover Return newly produced genotype data and provenance, or report inapplicability. Assign offspring IDs, parentage, birth generation, and fresh evaluation state. Termination Decide whether finalized run state satisfies a stopping policy. Invoke the policy once per completed generation and record its decision and state. Payload codec Encode and decode application data without loss of required semantics. Record codec identity/version and coordinate durable storage. 6.2 Individual and population evaluation The conceptual interfaces are: evaluate(individual, context) -> EvaluationResult evaluate_population(population, context) -> results_associated_with_individual_ids An application may provide either or both hooks. When a population hook is configured, the engine uses it directly. Otherwise, a standard adapter invokes the individual hook. Supplying both must not cause duplicate evaluation. A population hook is allowed to evaluate candidates jointly, comparatively, or in a shared environment. It need not produce the same results as an independent loop. The Java application examples already use an override of whole-population evaluation through an application-specific batch system. J5 Before updating population state, the evaluation coordinator must verify that all requested IDs have exactly one accepted result, no unexpected IDs occur, and every fitness value is finite. A sequence-based result API must detect duplicate IDs; a mapping-based API must enforce complete expected key coverage. The framework increments accepted-evaluation counts consistently. One accepted fitness result counts as one fitness evaluation. An application performing several simulations or training iterations to produce that result reports those quantities separately, unless a separately specified evaluation-round policy schedules multiple fitness evaluations. Applications control their own execution mechanism behind the hook. The initial engine need not create process pools or remote workers. No outstanding jobs may remain at a generation checkpoint boundary. 6.3 Stateful components Components retaining information that affects future results must provide explicit state export and restoration, conceptually: state_dict() -> JSONValue load_state_dict(state: JSONValue) -> None The component composition must identify each stateful component with a stable key. Stateless functions require no state payload, but must depend only on their explicit inputs and controlled external conditions. Hidden mutable globals or closures must not be silently treated as checkpointable state. Adaptive operators, selectors, evaluators, stopping windows, decoding state, and future-affecting caches are included. A deterministic derived cache may be discarded and recomputed only when doing so cannot change randomness, counters, budgets, or subsequent behavior. Observation-only reporting hooks run after commit and must not mutate evolutionary state. An application hook that changes future behavior is a registered stateful component and must run at a defined point before the checkpoint. 7. Generation lifecycle and algorithms 7.1 Fresh initialization Load and validate the configuration envelope and establish run-owned randomness. Resolve defaults and any sampled initial settings once, validate the resulting effective settings, and retain their values. Construct experiment components from those settings, create initial genotype data, and assign IDs. Evaluate the initial population and validate the complete result set. Apply results, initialize tracking, perform registered adaptive updates, and evaluate the stopping policy once. Finalize generation 0 and commit its checkpoint and scientific records when persistent storage is enabled. A crash before the generation-0 checkpoint requires a fresh initialization. There is no earlier committed state to restore. 7.2 A generation transition Starting with completed generation g, snapshot its effective settings as the settings to use for generation g + 1: Select survivors from the evaluated previous population. The default retains none. Determine the number of children needed to reach the configured population size. Select parents from the complete previous population, including individuals not selected to survive. Produce each child through mutation or crossover, according to the configured mutation probability. Assemble survivors and children into the population for g + 1. Evaluate the complete resulting population, including survivors, and validate all results. Apply results in the defined order; update accounting, statistics, historical records, and registered adaptive state. Evaluate termination exactly once and retain both its decision and any updated predicate state. Commit the finalized generation and expose its immutable record to callers. The Java implementation explicitly keeps genomes marked for removal eligible as parents and evaluates the resulting population after replacement. These intended semantics are preserved. J6 No partial offspring population or partially applied evaluation batch may be committed as a completed generation. Failure before commit stops the transition; recovery uses the last committed state. The framework does not promise automatic in-memory rollback of arbitrary application objects after a hook fails. Post-evaluation adaptive hooks may update future policy and component state, but must not rewrite the population or accepted observations being finalized. Changes to population-construction and evaluation settings take effect on the next transition. Preserve a generation_settings snapshot for the completed generation separately from the effective_settings prepared for the next one. For example, increasing the target size from 100 to 120 leaves the completed population at 100 and schedules 120 for its successor. 7.3 Default policies Policy Initial behavior Population size Positive configured target size; default 100. Survivor policy Complete replacement by default; simple elitist survival is also available. Parent pool Entire evaluated previous population. Parent sampling With replacement; self-crossover is permitted. Variation route Mutation or crossover for each child; default mutation probability 0.5. Evaluation Evaluate the full resulting population every generation. Objective Maximize one finite scalar fitness. Parent selector Roulette by default; uniform and tournament selectors are available. Termination Explicit configured predicates, including logical combinations. Mutation probability is the probability of choosing the mutation route. It is not an independent post-crossover mutation probability. Population size and mutation-probability defaults match the base Java configuration. J7 7.4 Selection and numerical validity The data model accepts finite signed fitness. Roulette requires finite nonnegative selection weights. With the default direct fitness-to-weight policy, a negative score is an error that explains the need for a compatible selector or an explicit weight transformation. An all-zero roulette population selects uniformly as a documented policy. Do not silently switch to uniform selection because one score is negative. Tournament selection compares signed scalar scores directly. Any future transformation of scores into weights must be explicit because it can change selection pressure. Reject NaN, infinity, invalid weights, and empty parent pools before sampling. Define stable tie-breaking by ID where ordering is required. Equal fitness must not count as an improvement solely because a different ID wins a tie. 7.5 Bounded operator attempts For one offspring attempt: Choose the variation route and parent or parents. Try positive-weight operators in weighted order without replacement. Try any explicitly registered fallbacks in their documented order. If all fail with a recoverable inapplicability result, retry with new parents subject to a finite per-child attempt budget. If the budget is exhausted, raise a descriptive reproduction error before committing the generation. Zero-weight operators are disabled in the weighted pool. A fallback must be declared explicitly. This replaces Java's implicit availability of zero-weight operators after positive choices are exhausted. Only a specific inapplicability exception or result is recoverable. Unexpected programming errors propagate. Validate configuration so that every route with nonzero probability has an operator or declared fallback. Zero-probability routes may have empty pools. The implementation must not retain a partly exhausted operator pool across unrelated offspring attempts. The existing Java reproduction loop can retry indefinitely when no operator succeeds; the Python attempt budget corrects that behavior. J8, J9 7.6 Stopping and limits Generation 0 does not count as an evolutionary transition. A maximum-generation limit of N stops no later than the completion of generation N, unless another predicate stops earlier. Evaluation-count predicates check accepted fitness evaluations at completed-generation boundaries. A threshold can be crossed while completing a population; this is not a hard mid-batch spending cap. Exact pre-operation resource budgeting is outside the initial execution contract. Persist stateful stopping windows, counters, and the resulting StopDecision. Restoration must not evaluate the stopping policy again for the saved generation. A checkpoint whose saved decision is terminated remains terminated under ordinary resume. 8. JSON configuration 8.1 Configuration envelope JSON describes settings and component identities; Python application code provides behavior. A proposed complete arithmetic example is: { \"schema_version\": 1, \"seed\": 12345, \"experiment\": { \"factory\": \"examples.arithmetic:build_experiment\", \"parameters\": { \"target\": 64 } }, \"evolution\": { \"population_size\": 100, \"mutation_probability\": 0.5, \"parent_selection\": { \"name\": \"roulette\" }, \"survival\": { \"name\": \"replace_all\" }, \"max_attempts_per_child\": 100 }, \"termination\": { \"any\": [ { \"max_generations\": 200 }, { \"fitness_at_least\": 1.0 } ] } } For this example, the application can define fitness as 1 / (1 + absolute_error), making 1.0 the exact-target score. Operator definitions and application-specific parameters are constructed by examples.arithmetic:build_experiment. The general configuration does not require a universal declarative schema for arbitrary Python objects. 8.2 Validation and effective settings Configuration handling must: Reject unsupported schema versions, duplicate JSON object keys, unknown core fields, invalid types, and out-of-range settings. Reject non-finite numbers. Python's JSON implementation accepts some nonstandard numeric values by default, so strict encoding and decoding must be configured explicitly. P4 Validate application parameters through the application factory or its registered validator. Resolve defaults and optional sampled values once, then record the effective settings. Preserve bounds and explicit mutability/update policies where the application uses them. Ordinary invalid values must not be silently clamped. Make runtime parameter changes explicit, validated, and recorded before the next commit. Save both the completed generation's settings snapshot and the effective settings prepared for the next transition, as defined in Section 7.2. Reconstruct resumed runs from saved settings and component identities rather than current defaults. Input configuration and Python objects may both be supported as entry points. Programmatic callers must receive equivalent validation of core invariants. 8.3 Factory resolution An explicit application-supplied registry may map short names to factories. An explicit import reference such as package.module:build_experiment may also be used. Automatic scanning of installed packages is unnecessary for the initial version. Factories construct compatible components from validated settings. Stored identities, component schema versions, and relevant implementation fingerprints support compatibility checking on restoration. Component construction must be separate from executing the fresh-run initializer. 9. Randomness and deterministic execution 9.1 Persistent random state Use per-run generators rather than module-global random calls. Preserve separate evolution and evaluation randomness so additional evaluation work does not silently change the variation stream. Save the complete state of every persistent generator. Python's Random.getstate() and setstate() support this; the saved object includes details such as any cached Gaussian value. Saving only the original seed is insufficient. P5 Record generator identity and any seed-derivation version. Restoration must preserve required types rather than treating every JSON array as an interchangeable Python list. 9.2 Stable work identities Individual evaluation identities should include generation, individual ID, evaluation round, and replicate where applicable. Whole-population evaluators receive a stable batch identity and ordered population view. Derive evaluation seeds from a saved root seed and a stable, versioned encoding of the logical work identity. Do not derive them from worker numbers, completion order, wall-clock time, or Python's process-dependent hash(). Do not use a randomly generated run ID in seed derivation if two fresh runs with the same root seed and inputs are intended to reproduce one another. Run IDs may still identify journal records and external idempotency keys. 9.3 Ordering Population iteration, operator ordering, tie-breaking, ID assignment, and result application must be deterministic. Numerical summaries must use a defined accumulation order. Parallel evaluators return results to the coordinator, which applies them in that order. Invariance to worker count additionally requires application independence and numerically compatible execution. A joint population evaluator may depend on grouping, so grouping is part of its policy and state. 9.4 Application responsibilities Application-owned random generators must either expose persistent state or initialize each independent job from its supplied context. The application must also restore any simulator, optimizer, curriculum, cache, or environment state that influences future results. Exact continuation is conditional on compatible code, data, numerical settings, and execution environment. Python documents that many higher-level random algorithms can change across versions. Numerical libraries can impose additional limits; PyTorch, for example, does not promise identical results across releases, platforms, or CPU/GPU execution simply from matching seeds. P5, P6 10. Checkpoint and recovery contract 10.1 Required guarantee Given compatible application code and execution conditions, and components satisfying the explicit state and randomness contracts, restoring a committed checkpoint continues the same logical evolutionary run. The guarantee concerns genotypes, population membership, fitness observations, selection and variation behavior, identities, counters, stopping behavior, and committed scientific history. Process IDs, diagnostic timestamps, downtime, and console-output timing may differ. Default persistent execution must checkpoint after every completed generation, including generation 0. In-memory execution may omit persistence entirely. 10.2 Exact saved boundary A checkpoint represents the point after all of the following: All scheduled evaluations for the generation have finished. Results have been validated and applied in deterministic order. Population membership, counters, metrics, genealogy records, and historical bests agree. Registered adaptive updates have run exactly once. The stopping policy has run exactly once, and its updated state and decision have been retained. It represents the point before any work for the next generation. There are no outstanding jobs or partially applied mutations. Its phase is ready for the next generation or terminated. Restoration must not repeat initialization, evaluate the saved generation, repeat its adaptive updates, rerun its stateful stopping policy, or replay mutating callbacks. A ready checkpoint at generation g proceeds to g + 1; a terminated checkpoint remains terminated. 10.3 Required checkpoint contents State Reason Run and journal identity; completed generation; phase; stop decision and reason Identifies the authoritative run and its next permitted transition. Ordered population; stable IDs; complete genotype payloads; observations; birth and parent information Restores the actual candidates and deterministic iteration order. Next individual, evaluation, and event identifiers Prevents reuse and changes in logical work identities. Accepted-evaluation counts and logical budgets Preserves stopping and adaptation behavior. Completed-generation settings, next-transition effective settings, and resolved component identities Preserves the meaning of the saved population and prevents default changes or resampling from altering continuation. All persistent RNG states and evaluation-seed metadata Restores the current position of each random stream. Historical-best snapshots, last-improvement generation, and policy-dependent archives or windows Preserves information absent from the current population. State of all future-affecting application components Restores adaptive probabilities, retained evaluation state, and meaningful caches. Committed journal byte offset, next event sequence, and integrity information Keeps durable history aligned with the checkpoint. Checkpoint schema, codec IDs/versions, application identities, and relevant runtime/environment fingerprints Supports explicit compatibility checks. 10.4 Serialization and attachments Checkpoint encoding must not mutate the run, advance its random streams, or trigger evaluation. Application state methods and codecs have the same restriction. JSON-native payloads are supported initially. The codec contract must permit future binary attachments without requiring large arrays or models to be expanded into JSON lists. An attachment reference must identify its format/version and integrity information. Required attachments must be durable before the checkpoint that references them is committed. Components are reconstructed from identities and settings, then their explicit state is restored. Install saved persistent RNG states after reconstruction so setup work cannot advance the restored streams. Components must retain references to the managed generators that receive those states. Do not make arbitrary closure or entire Python object-graph serialization the default mechanism. If a configured component or payload cannot satisfy its checkpoint contract, fail clearly before claiming a durable checkpoint. 10.5 Single-writer commit protocol One coordinator owns commits for a run. Workers return evaluation results and do not append to the authoritative journal or replace checkpoints. The local-file implementation must: Finalize and validate generation state in memory; prepare its immutable scientific records and required payload representations. Make required immutable attachments durable, if any. Append the generation's records to history.jsonl; flush and synchronize the journal. Write a temporary checkpoint in the same filesystem as the active checkpoint. Include the committed journal byte offset, identity, integrity information, next event sequence, and finalized state. Flush and synchronize the temporary checkpoint, then atomically replace checkpoint.json. Synchronize affected directory entries where supported when establishing the documented local durability guarantee. Report commit success and generate optional projections from committed records. Python exposes flush()/os.fsync() for buffered-file synchronization and os.replace() for atomic replacement when successful. Replacement may fail across filesystems, so temporary and final files must share a filesystem. P7 Atomic publication and power-loss durability are separate milestones. After successful replacement, readers in the running system observe the new checkpoint; an ordinary process crash can recover it because its journal prefix and payloads were synchronized first. A machine or power failure before directory synchronization may recover the old or new checkpoint on filesystems supporting this protocol. Recovery must validate whichever committed checkpoint is present. For generation 0, there may be no surviving checkpoint yet. Do not claim power-loss durability until the required synchronization has succeeded; document a narrower guarantee on platforms that cannot provide it. The checkpoint is the authority for the committed journal prefix. A failed write must not be reported as a successful commit. Retrying a write must not rerun generation finalization or create duplicate committed events; an implementation may instead stop with the preceding checkpoint still authoritative. 10.6 Restoration and incomplete writes On resume: Load the explicit active checkpoint and validate its schema, identities, and compatibility requirements. Verify the required journal prefix and attachments. Ignore or remove bytes after the saved journal offset before any new append. They belong to uncommitted work. Fail if committed history or required attachments are missing or corrupt. Do not repair scientific state by reevaluating saved individuals. Reconstruct components, restore all state, and install saved random states. Honor the saved stop decision and phase, then continue only if the saved run is ready. If the process failed during the next generation, work after the last commit may repeat using the same logical identities and random contexts. No committed generation is reevaluated. A checkpoint copied to another location is a bundle: checkpoint state plus its required journal prefix and attachments. A JSON manifest alone is resumable only when it contains or can resolve everything required. 10.7 Limits of the guarantee External calls may repeat if they completed before a crash but their results were not committed. Applications requiring deduplication must use an external idempotency or result-retrieval mechanism. Resuming inside a long evaluation requires that evaluator to preserve its own partial computation. Model training may require model, optimizer, scheduler, data-position, and application RNG state. Nondeterministic numerical operations or changing external data can change future results even when framework state is restored correctly. A time-based policy may restore an active-runtime budget excluding downtime, but cannot generally promise the same final generation because execution durations can change. Arbitrary external side effects and console messages do not inherit exactly-once semantics from local journal commits. These limits must be documented as application contracts rather than reasons to block the generic implementation. Completed-generation continuation is the required initial capability. 11. Tracking, genealogy, and reporting 11.1 Scientific records Retain headless population analysis and genealogy. The Java tools already derive population summaries and genealogy from saved populations independently of the graphical interface. J10 The structured history must support: Population membership identifiers and generation-level fitness summaries. Supplementary metric summaries, including contributing counts. Accepted evaluation observations needed by the supplied reports. Birth generation, parent IDs, operator identity, and operation details. Independent historical-best snapshots and last-improvement information. Relevant effective-setting changes and stopping decisions. Each event has a stable run/generation/sequence identity. Store parent links as the authoritative lineage relationship; derive child indexes from them rather than maintaining two independently mutable directions. An individual's evaluation observations may change over its lifetime. Its birth record must not treat first-observed fitness as permanent fitness. 11.2 Statistics and historical bests Compute min, mean, and max for fitness and supplied numeric metrics, with observation counts. Use a stable tie-breaker for presentation and selection ordering. A best-fitness improvement requires a strictly better fitness value under the configured objective comparison; identity alone does not establish improvement. Maintain independent historical-best state, including candidates no longer in the population. Do not retain references that allow live mutation to rewrite earlier records. This corrects problematic coupling in the Java statistics implementation. J11 11.3 Report projections Only committed records contribute to durable scientific reports. analyze must be idempotent: running it repeatedly over the same committed prefix produces the same logical output. JSON exports are required. CSV summaries may be provided as a convenience. Report generation must not consume evolutionary randomness, alter component state, or invoke evaluation. Operational progress messages may differ after recovery. The authoritative scientific history must not duplicate committed generations or births. 12. Command-line interface and run directories 12.1 Commands Command Behavior xevolution run --config CONFIG --output RUN_DIR Creates a fresh run, resolves the experiment, initializes generation 0, and evolves. Refuses to silently reuse an existing populated run directory. xevolution resume RUN_DIR Restores checkpoint.json and the recorded component composition, repairs only uncommitted journal tail as specified, and continues from the saved phase. xevolution inspect RUN_DIR Displays effective settings, progress, best results, and checkpoint metadata without evolving the population. xevolution analyze RUN_DIR --output REPORT_DIR Builds population summaries and genealogy exports from committed history without evaluating candidates. python -m xevolution must expose the same commands. CLI functions return exit codes or raise errors handled at the command boundary. Library code must not terminate the interpreter or reconfigure an application's logging. Ordinary resume uses saved effective settings. It must not silently accept changed defaults or change an evolutionary policy. Explicitly changing a saved run's policies would be a separately identified continuation/fork operation, outside the initial four-command interface. 12.2 Run directory contents Path Contents config.json Resolved starting configuration. Current effective settings are also recorded in checkpoints and relevant journal events. checkpoint.json Latest authoritative checkpoint. history.jsonl Structured generation, measurement, membership, and lineage records. The checkpoint identifies its committed prefix. artifacts/ Immutable payload attachments supplied by codecs, when used. reports/ Rebuildable analysis exports. Inspection and analysis read a specific committed prefix. They must not treat uncommitted journal tail or the most recently modified arbitrary file as authoritative. A graceful stop request should finish the current generation and save at the defined boundary. An abrupt failure may require replaying work since the last commit. Failure to save must be reported as a failure to make the new state durable. An operational pause, such as a graceful user interrupt, must not by itself set the evolutionary stop decision to terminated. Save a ready checkpoint unless the configured stopping policy has independently finished the run. This distinction allows ordinary resume after a planned pause. 13. Java functionality and utility migration 13.1 Main functionality mapping Java area Python destination genetics interfaces and implementations Shared records in models.py; initialization, cloning, and optional decoding contracts in protocols.py; application composition in experiment.py. Evolution and reproduction strategies engine.py, reproduction.py, and the experiment composition. Evaluation strategies and genome decoders evaluation.py plus application-supplied evaluation/decoding functions or objects. Survival filters Survivor-selection contract and policies in selection.py. Operators and operator selectors operators.py, selection.py, and the finite attempt workflow in reproduction.py. Evolution configuration and stopping conditions config.py, registry.py, and termination.py. Experiment and population-analysis tools cli.py, experiment.py, storage.py, and tracking.py. Population statistics and genealogy data tracking.py, immutable records in models.py, and the committed journal. GUI package Excluded by agreed scope. 13.2 Xnet utility behavior Java utility or pattern Python replacement XId and ID allocation Explicit run and individual identity plus saved counters in models/run state. XValId, ValueMap, and primitive type dispatch Typed core fields, named metric and metadata mappings, and boundary validation. XIdObjectManager Ordinary mappings for lookup and genealogy queries in tracking.py. RandomUtil randomness.py, with selection policies in selection.py and operator ordering in operators.py. Configuration builders, fields, ranges, and vetting Typed settings and explicit validation/sampling/update behavior in config.py. Condition and combinators Callable stopping predicates and logical composition in termination.py. XML formats and readers/writers Versioned codecs in serialization.py and durable operations in storage.py. XLogger Standard logging configured by the application or CLI. XOption subclasses CLI argument handling. Collection builders and transforms Collection literals, comprehensions, iterators, and functions where used. WormField lazy construction Explicit experiment construction and local caching only when needed. Validation hierarchies Focused checks and exceptions in errors.py. The Java configuration machinery supports initial ranges, lifetime bounds, and mutability rules. Preserve any such behavior used by the port through explicit operations; its inheritance hierarchy is not required. J12 13.3 Documented behavior changes docs/markdown/porting_notes.md must identify at least these changes: JSON and explicit checkpoint state replace Java XML and class-name serialization. Fresh run and resume are separate paths; resume does not reinitialize the population. J14 Operator attempts are finite, and fallback operators are explicit. Invalid or incompatible roulette weights fail clearly; negative fitness no longer silently disables selection pressure. J13 Individual IDs come from saved allocation state. Newborn age is zero, derived from birth generation. Missing metrics are excluded from each metric's denominator and reported with contributing counts. Historical records are independent snapshots. Evaluation bookkeeping has one framework owner. Scientific history and checkpoints share a committed-generation boundary. 14. Examples and documentation 14.1 Example modules Module Purpose and interactions examples/__init__.py Makes example factories importable when running from the repository. examples/arithmetic.py Demonstrates a string-based representation, initialization, optional decoding, individual scoring, mutation, crossover, and a JSON-native codec through the public experiment API. examples/batch_problem.py Demonstrates whole-population evaluation, stable result identities, and a joint evaluation contract. It must use the population hook directly. Provide examples/arithmetic.json and examples/batch_problem.json, plus examples/README.md with setup and commands. Examples should remain small and independent of NEAT or the excluded simulation subsystems. The approved Digits extension adds examples/neural_digits.py, normal and quick JSON presets, and examples/digits_report.py. It evolves 1–3 hidden layers with 8, 16, 32, or 64 neurons per layer and ReLU/tanh activation. Fitness is solely validation accuracy, maximized, with no loss or model-size penalty. Each candidate receives a fresh CPU-trained MLP with a fixed training budget and recorded data and training seeds. The default stratified split is 1,000 training, 400 validation, and 397 final test examples. The final test partition is excluded from evolution and used by the separate report only after termination. Numerical libraries belong to an optional neural uv dependency group; core runtime dependencies remain empty. This application must satisfy the same completed- generation checkpoint and explicit-state contracts as other applications. See docs/markdown/digits_demo.md for the implemented application contract and presets. A repository-root development invocation may use: uv run python -m xevolution run --config examples/arithmetic.json --output runs/arithmetic uv run python -m xevolution resume runs/arithmetic uv run python -m xevolution inspect runs/arithmetic uv run python -m xevolution analyze runs/arithmetic --output runs/arithmetic/reports resume continues only if the saved run is ready for further evolution. A run that already satisfied its stopping policy is inspected or analyzed without further evolution. 14.2 Documentation files File Required contents docs/markdown/index.md Documentation home and reading routes. docs/markdown/overview.md What the package does, its central concepts, application responsibilities, and generation lifecycle. docs/markdown/examples.md Comparison and entry points for all three examples. docs/markdown/arithmetic_demo.md Bit-string encoding, decoding, scoring, operators, configuration, and commands. docs/markdown/batch_demo.md Shared-resource scoring, genuine whole-population evaluation, contextual fitness, configuration, and commands. docs/markdown/digits_demo.md MLP architecture, fixed training recipe, accuracy fitness, presets, reporting, and continuation. docs/markdown/building_docs.md Markdown maintenance, HTML generation, and documentation validation. docs/markdown/architecture.md Module responsibilities, data ownership, generation lifecycle, and dependency boundaries. docs/markdown/extensions.md Application factory, genotype ownership/cloning, individual and population evaluation, custom selection/operators, component state, and codecs. docs/markdown/configuration.md JSON envelope, built-in policies, defaults, validation, sampled/effective settings, and stopping semantics. docs/markdown/checkpointing.md Exact saved boundary, required state, commit/recovery protocol, bundle contents, compatibility, and application limits. docs/markdown/cli.md Commands, run directory contents, stopping, resume, inspection, and analysis. docs/markdown/porting_notes.md Java-to-Python mapping, excluded areas, and deliberate corrections or behavior changes. Generate the HTML edition under docs/html/ from these Markdown sources. Local navigation, page links, and heading fragments must work after extracting a documentation-only archive. Include all reading assets locally and keep application commands explicitly relative to the source project root. AGENTS.md must direct coding agents to preserve these contracts, keep application assumptions outside the engine, use explicit run-owned randomness, and verify meaningful end-to-end behavior. It should specify the project-local environment and normal build/test commands once implemented. 15. Verification and acceptance criteria 15.1 Test modules Module Primary coverage tests/conftest.py Reusable deterministic components, stateful test applications, payload codecs, and temporary run stores. tests/test_evolution.py Population size, survivor/parent behavior, mutation-or-crossover routing, identity, finite retries, generation/age semantics, and stopping. tests/test_evaluation.py Individual adaptation, genuine population evaluation, result association and validation, accepted-evaluation accounting, and deterministic application order. tests/test_tracking.py Historical snapshots, missing-metric statistics, genealogy after ancestors leave the population, and idempotent analysis. tests/test_resume.py Uninterrupted execution versus new-process restoration, including stateful extensions and custom codecs. tests/test_storage_recovery.py Failures around journal writes, attachment preparation, and checkpoint replacement; missing/corrupt committed data; safe uncommitted-tail recovery. tests/test_cli.py Fresh run, explicit resume, inspection, analysis, errors, and the installed entry points. Tests should verify observable contracts rather than mirror helper implementations. Model, configuration, numerical, and serialization checks may be grouped into these behavioral suites or split into focused modules if needed. 15.2 Decisive continuation test Execute the same deterministic experiment in two ways: Run continuously from generation 0 through generation N. Run through generation K, commit, terminate the process, restore in a new process, and continue through generation N. Compare complete logical state: ordered populations, genotypes, fitness, metrics, IDs, counters, effective settings, RNG states, component states, historical bests, stopping decisions, and committed scientific records. Normalize only intentionally variable diagnostic metadata such as timestamps and fresh-run identity. Repeat with K = 0, with a later K, and with a failure during an uncommitted generation. Include a stateful evaluator or operator and a stateful stopping predicate, so repeating finalization or losing hidden state is observable. 15.3 Required acceptance cases ID Acceptance criterion A01 The package installs and imports on Python 3.12 without requiring a neural-network or simulation library. A02 Both examples run through the public application API; the batch example exercises whole-population evaluation directly. A03 Survivors are chosen separately from parents; the full previous population remains the default parent pool. A04 Every committed generation has valid fitness for all members, unique IDs, and the target population size recorded in that generation's settings snapshot. Adaptive setting updates affect the next transition. A05 Negative, non-finite, and all-zero weight cases follow documented policies without an infinite loop or silent loss of selection pressure. A06 Unusable operators exhaust a finite budget and raise a useful error; no partial generation is committed. A07 Modifying or reevaluating live individuals cannot alter earlier history or retained best snapshots. A08 Restoring generation 0 or a later generation reproduces the uninterrupted logical run in a new process under the determinism contract. A09 Saved-generation initialization, evaluation, adaptive updates, and stateful stopping are not repeated after restoration. A10 Recovery never exposes a partial committed generation. Process-failure visibility and power-loss durability follow Section 10.5; the new commit is durably guaranteed only after the required synchronization completes. A11 Uncommitted journal tail can be recovered safely; missing or corrupt committed history or attachments fails explicitly. A12 Repeated analysis of the same committed prefix does not duplicate generations or genealogy records. A13 Effective settings, component identities, codec versions, and complete random state are restored rather than inferred from current defaults. A14 The CLI distinguishes fresh creation, resume, inspection, and analysis and never silently resumes an arbitrary newest file. 16. Implementation sequence Increment 1: In-memory evolution Implement shared records and contracts, validated programmatic settings, initialization, explicit randomness, selection, operators, reproduction, evaluation, tracking needed by stopping, and the incremental engine. Complete the arithmetic example and core behavioral tests. The engine must already expose the intended completed-generation boundary, even before durable storage is implemented. Increment 2: Reliable continuation Implement state export/restoration, codecs, effective-configuration persistence, the journal/checkpoint commit protocol, compatibility checks, and recovery. Complete new-process equivalence tests and failure-injection cases before relying on saved experiments. Checkpointing belongs early enough that ownership mistakes can still be corrected without disrupting a large set of applications. Increment 3: Headless tools and documentation Complete JSON-driven factory construction, command-line commands, the whole-population example, analysis exports, packaging checks, and extension documentation. Verify both examples through the public CLI. Initial implementation is complete when All agreed capabilities are implemented, required acceptance cases pass, the documented command interface works from an installed package, and the checkpoint documentation accurately states both its guarantees and application responsibilities. Changes beyond the agreed scope should be proposed separately rather than silently incorporated into the core. 17. Source references The Java review was static source-and-test inspection of the pinned commit below. The legacy Java application was not built or executed. Architectural decisions and Python requirements in this document are recommendations for the new implementation, not claims that those features already exist in the repository. Java baseline and implementation evidence J1: Reviewed repository commit. J2: Xnet repository overview and subproject boundaries. J3: xevolution Maven dependencies. J4: Generic Java Genome contract. J5: Application-level whole-population evaluation example. J6: Java generation lifecycle. J7: Base evolution configuration and defaults. J8: Reproduction, parent selection, and retry loop. J9: Operator selection and reset behavior. J10: Headless population and genealogy data tracking. J11: Population statistics and historical-extrema handling. J12: Range-based configuration parameter behavior. J13: Java weight normalization. J14: Java fresh-start and population-loading behavior. Python and tooling references P1: uv project structure, environments, and lockfiles. P2: Python 3.12 typing and protocols. P3: Python Packaging User Guide: src layout. P4: Python 3.12 JSON encoding and decoding. P5: Python 3.12 random state and reproducibility. P6: PyTorch reproducibility limitations. P7: Python 3.12 file synchronization and replacement operations.","order":13},{"title":"Contributor and coding-agent guidelines","url":"contributing.html","text":"Contributor and coding-agent guidelines This is a documentation copy of the repository-root AGENTS.md, which remains the instruction file for agents working on the source project. Repository paths below are relative to that project root. See the package overview for an introduction and documentation maintenance for the Markdown/HTML workflow. This file guides coding agents working anywhere in this repository. Paths are relative to the repository root. The port specification defines the required behavior; this file defines the working practices and recommended implementation stack. Keep both consistent when requirements change. Inspect the checkout before acting. The specification describes a target architecture, so a named module, test, or command may not have been implemented yet. Do not report planned functionality as existing or verified functionality. 1. Project overview and scope Port the generic Java xevolution framework into a standalone, headless Python library for evolutionary computation. Preserve its useful capabilities, correct the identified algorithmic defects, and make application extension straightforward. The reviewed Java baseline is xtructure/xnet-public at commit 509c673e502f0c27daa6b91f39cc298404b59ae2. The agreed scope is: Python 3.12 and later, with an installable src/xevolution/ package. Arbitrary application-defined genotypes, individual evaluation, and genuine whole-population evaluation. One finite scalar objective, maximized by default, with extensible parent and survivor selection. Initialization, mutation, crossover, population replacement, termination, statistics, historical results, genealogy, and headless analysis. Versioned JSON configuration and explicit Python checkpoint state. Reliable continuation from completed-generation checkpoints under the specified state and determinism contracts. Necessary Xnet utility behavior incorporated into this package using suitable Python facilities. NEAT, the graphical interface, the operator workbench, and compatibility with Java files or random trajectories are outside this port. Do not introduce a built-in simulator, distributed scheduler, GPU requirement, or multiobjective engine without a requirement for that work. Applications can supply specialized capabilities through the extension contracts. 2. Recommended technology stack Area Project choice Language Python >=3.12; use syntax and standard-library APIs available at the minimum supported version. Environment and dependency management uv, a repository-local .venv, pyproject.toml, and a committed uv.lock. Core runtime Standard library initially. Prefer dataclasses, typing, collections.abc, random, math, json, pathlib, hashlib, logging, and argparse as appropriate. Packaging Hatchling build backend, with src/xevolution explicitly selected for the wheel and py.typed included. Build through uv build. Testing pytest, deterministic fixtures, temporary run directories, and subprocess-based resume tests. Formatting and linting Ruff formatter and linter. Static typing mypy, with strict checking for the library. Persistence Versioned JSON checkpoints and JSON Lines scientific history; application codecs for payloads. Documentation Markdown, executable examples, public API docstrings, and this repository-level instruction file. CI GitHub Actions using the same environment and check commands as local development. Test Python 3.12 and each newer version advertised as supported. Keep the initial runtime dependency list empty. Testing, linting, typing, and build tools are development/build dependencies. Numerical libraries belong to applications or justified optional integrations. Avoid adding a framework or dependency for behavior the standard library already handles clearly. 3. Required uv environment policy All Python dependencies must be installed in virtual environments created and managed by uv. Runtime, development, testing, documentation, and build dependencies are all covered by this requirement. Use the repository's .venv for normal development and execution. Temporary build-isolation or packaging-test environments are permitted when uv creates and manages them. Never install project dependencies into a system interpreter, a user-wide Python installation, or an unrelated environment. The standalone uv executable itself is tooling, not a project Python dependency. Run project Python commands and development tools through uv run. Before dependency-changing commands, verify that UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT is unset or resolves to this repository's .venv. Correct an unexpected override first; do not use --active to select an unrelated environment. Declare dependencies in pyproject.toml; commit the corresponding uv.lock changes. Use uv add, uv remove, and uv sync for ordinary dependency management. Do not maintain an unrecorded collection of manually installed packages. Do not use global pip, pip install --user, sudo pip, or uv pip --system. Do not use uvx or globally installed tools to bypass the project's locked development tools. Do not redirect the project environment to a system or shared interpreter. Configure the editor to use this repository's .venv. Do not commit .venv, caches, build output, generated run directories, or large checkpoint payloads. uv maintains the project environment and supports dependency groups; keep the normal developer tools in the dev group. See the official project workflow and dependency documentation. Initial project bootstrap Only when the Python project does not yet exist, create its package skeleton and pyproject.toml with requires-python = \">=3.12\", a Hatchling build-system declaration, the console entry point, and the tool settings below. Do not reinitialize or overwrite an existing project. Use Python 3.12 as the initial development baseline: uv python install 3.12 uv python pin 3.12 uv add --group dev pytest ruff mypy uv sync --group dev The uv add command creates or updates the lockfile. Commit pyproject.toml, uv.lock, and .python-version. If editing dependency declarations directly during an intentional dependency change, run uv lock and review its diff. Existing checkout Run from the repository root: uv sync --locked --group dev uv run --locked --group dev python -c \"import sys; from pathlib import Path; assert sys.prefix != sys.base_prefix; assert Path(sys.prefix).resolve() == Path('.venv').resolve(); print(sys.executable)\" The interpreter should belong to the repository's .venv. An explicit environment activation is unnecessary for these commands. --locked makes stale or missing lock state an error instead of silently updating it. Resolve the actual dependency mismatch; do not remove the flag merely to make a check pass. See locking and syncing. Hatchling belongs in [build-system].requires, with backend hatchling.build. Let uv manage isolated build dependencies. Do not assume that uv.lock alone pins every isolated build requirement; use deliberate build constraints when reproducible distribution builds are needed. See uv builds and Hatchling package selection. 4. Repository and module boundaries Follow the shallow module layout in the specification. Organize by responsibility, not by Java class hierarchy. Location Ownership models.py, protocols.py, errors.py Shared records, extension contracts, and focused exceptions. No dependency on concrete engine, storage, or CLI implementations. config.py, randomness.py Validated settings, effective configuration, managed random streams, and stable evaluation contexts. evaluation.py, selection.py, operators.py, reproduction.py, termination.py Evolutionary policies and calculations. Framework bookkeeping has one explicit owner. engine.py, experiment.py, registry.py Lifecycle coordination, component composition, and explicit factory resolution. serialization.py Conversion between typed state and versioned representations. storage.py File operations, journal positions, checkpoint publication, and recovery. tracking.py Scientific statistics, historical snapshots, genealogy, and report projections. cli.py, __main__.py, __init__.py Command boundaries and a small public import API. examples/, tests/, docs/ Application examples, behavioral verification, and user/developer documentation. The Python-module rows above are under src/xevolution/. The engine must not import example applications. Application behavior enters through experiment composition and explicit factories. Keep optional application imports out of package initialization. Documentation prose lives in docs/markdown/; docs/html/ is generated with docs/build_docs.py. Update Markdown first, keep all three example guides and the overview consistent with actual behavior, and verify local links and heading fragments after rebuilding HTML. The documentation builder uses the standard library and the standalone Pandoc executable. Do not recreate Xnet as a dependency. Port needed utility behavior into the module that owns it; avoid a miscellaneous utils.py that accumulates unrelated responsibilities. 5. Python coding practices API and type design Prefer small functions, explicit collaborators, and cohesive records. Use classes for meaningful state and behavior. Use typing.Protocol and well-typed callables for extension points. Applications should not need to inherit from framework base classes. Avoid interface/abstract/implementation triples, routine builders, getter/setter wrappers, service locators, and one-class-per-file layouts. Annotate public APIs and core internal interfaces. Preserve genotype generics across component boundaries. Use modern built-in annotations such as list[T] and T | None, with Python 3.12-compatible generic syntax. Prefer Sequence, Mapping, or other appropriate abstract interfaces for inputs when their operations suffice. Do not expose mutable coordinator collections unnecessarily. Validate untrusted or application-provided values at boundaries, then work with typed records. Type annotations and runtime protocol checks do not validate result contents. Keep Any, casts, and type-checker suppressions narrow and explain them. Python typing documentation Use keyword-only parameters where several booleans, probabilities, or optional policies would make positional calls unclear. Avoid hidden behavior encoded in loosely related flags. Data ownership and arbitrary genotypes Keep genotype type G unconstrained. Framework code must not assume it supports hashing, equality, ordering, truth testing, length, indexing, or numerical-array operations. Identify individuals through explicit framework IDs. Do not let dataclass-generated equality, ordering, or hashing traverse genotype payloads. Configure those methods deliberately and use explicit selection keys. Use default_factory for mutable dataclass defaults; never use shared mutable function defaults. Prefer frozen records for retained observations and value-like configuration where appropriate, but remember that a frozen wrapper does not freeze a nested genotype. dataclasses.replace() is not a cloning policy. Use application cloning/codecs for independent offspring and historical snapshots. Dataclass semantics Mutators and crossovers must leave parents unchanged and return independent children unless the shared data is explicitly immutable. Fresh offspring receive fresh IDs and evaluation bookkeeping. Avoid blanket deep-copying of arbitrary large objects. Define ownership and copy only what the application contract requires. Keep large payloads out of routine representations and log messages. Readability, errors, and resources Use snake_case for functions/modules, PascalCase for classes, and clear domain names. Let Ruff enforce formatting; prefer readable control flow over clever expressions. Document public contracts, units, side effects, ownership, failure modes, and determinism requirements. Comments should explain decisions and invariants. Raise focused exceptions with useful context and preserve causes with exception chaining. Catch only the defined operator-inapplicability condition for evolutionary retries; unexpected exceptions must remain failures. Do not use assert to validate configuration, evaluator results, or checkpoint data. Assertions are appropriate for internal invariants and tests. Use pathlib, context managers, and explicit UTF-8 text encoding. Keep scientific persistence in storage; components must not write their own checkpoint files. Use logging.getLogger(__name__) in library modules. Configure logging and process exit only at the application/CLI boundary. Importing the package must not start work, consume randomness, or create run files. Profile a concrete bottleneck before optimizing. Preserve stable ordering and state ownership when changing performance-sensitive code. 6. Evolutionary invariants Preserve these behaviors when implementing or refactoring; the specification supplies the full algorithms and defaults: Generation 0 is the evaluated initial population. Newborn age is zero; derive age from birth generation. Allocate IDs with saved monotonic counters. Never reconstruct the next ID from current survivors. Represent unevaluated fitness explicitly, such as None; zero is valid fitness. Keep parent selection and survivor selection separate. The default parent pool includes the entire previous population, including nonsurvivors. Default to complete replacement and reevaluate the entire resulting population, including survivors. Mutation probability selects mutation or crossover for each child; it is not a second mutation stage after crossover. Use the whole-population evaluator directly when configured. Otherwise adapt the individual evaluator. Providing both must not evaluate candidates twice. Validate the complete evaluation result set before applying scores or accounting: every expected ID exactly once, no unexpected IDs, and finite fitness values. The framework owns accepted-evaluation counts. Signed fitness is valid. Direct roulette weighting requires nonnegative weights; all-zero weights select uniformly. Negative weights require an error or an explicitly configured transformation, never a silent fallback. Bound reproduction attempts. Try positive-weight operators without replacement within an attempt, then explicit fallbacks. Zero-weight operators are disabled in the weighted pool; explicitly declared fallbacks remain available. Unexpected errors are not retry signals. Keep historical snapshots independent of live state. Missing metrics are not zero; means use each metric's contributing count. Equal fitness with a different ID is not an improvement. Resolve sampled settings before constructing components. Preserve generation_settings separately from effective_settings prepared for the next transition. Adaptive hooks must not rewrite finalized observations or population data. 7. Determinism and checkpoint discipline Checkpoint compatibility and exact continuation are core behavior, not optional conveniences. Read the specification's checkpoint section before changing any stateful component, generation ordering, randomness, or storage behavior. Inject run-owned random generators and stable evaluation contexts. Do not use module-global randomness, wall time, process IDs, unordered set iteration, worker-completion order, or Python's randomized hash() for scientific decisions. Separate evolution and evaluation randomness. Persist complete generator states and versioned seed-derivation metadata, not only the initial seed. Preserve deterministic population, operator, tie-break, result-application, and numerical-accumulation order. Derive job randomness from stable logical work identities. Every component retaining future-affecting state must expose explicit export/restoration. Include adaptive policies, evaluator state, stopping windows, caches that affect behavior, and application RNGs. Hidden mutable globals and closures are not a checkpoint mechanism. Commit only after complete evaluation, validated result application, tracking, adaptive updates, and one stopping decision. There must be no jobs in flight. When persistent storage is enabled, checkpoint generation 0 and every later completed generation by default. Preserve in-memory execution without a run directory or file I/O. Persist ordered population payloads, identities/counters, both settings snapshots, RNGs, historical state, component states, termination state, journal position/integrity, and schema/codec/application compatibility information. Restoration reconstructs components, restores explicit state, and installs saved RNG state after setup. Do not rerun initialization, saved-generation evaluation, adaptive updates, or stateful stopping. A graceful operational pause leaves the checkpoint ready unless a configured stopping predicate terminates the run. Ordinary resume must honor a saved terminal decision. One coordinator commits: durable attachments first, then append/synchronize history, then write/synchronize a temporary checkpoint and atomically replace the active one. Complete required directory synchronization before claiming power-loss durability. The checkpoint identifies the committed journal prefix. Before writable resume, truncate or reset the journal to the saved committed byte offset; new appends must begin there. Read-only inspection may ignore the uncommitted tail. Missing or corrupt committed data is an error, not permission to reevaluate saved candidates. Encoding and post-commit reporting must not mutate evolutionary state or consume its randomness. Reports must be reproducible from committed records. Exact continuation assumes compatible application code, data, state, and numerical execution. Mid-generation external work may repeat after a crash; applications own any required idempotency. Do not equate support for several Python versions with exact checkpoint portability between them. 8. JSON and numerical boundaries Reject unsupported schema versions, unknown core configuration fields, duplicate object keys, invalid types, and out-of-range values. Application factories validate their own parameter namespaces. Validate programmatic entry points as well as JSON input. Explicitly reject booleans where integer IDs/counts or numeric probabilities/fitness are required. Reject non-finite values after parsing, including overflow such as 1e999, and encode strict JSON with allow_nan=False. Rejecting only the NaN and Infinity tokens is insufficient. Keep configuration, runtime records, and serialized payloads distinct. Use explicit codecs; do not treat dataclasses.asdict(), __dict__, pickle, or arbitrary object-graph serialization as the persistence design. Preserve tuple-based RNG state, application payload semantics, numeric precision, and string-key requirements explicitly. Include schema and codec versions; validate before accepting restored state. Make weight normalization and summaries numerically sound. Finite inputs can still produce invalid aggregates; validate results and use stable arithmetic where appropriate. 9. Tool configuration and normal checks Keep tool settings in pyproject.toml. Recommended starting configuration: [tool.ruff] target-version = \"py312\" line-length = 88 [tool.ruff.lint] select = [\"E4\", \"E7\", \"E9\", \"F\", \"I\", \"UP\", \"B\"] [tool.mypy] python_version = \"3.12\" strict = true [tool.pytest.ini_options] testpaths = [\"tests\"] addopts = [\"--import-mode=importlib\"] [tool.hatch.build.targets.wheel] packages = [\"src/xevolution\"] This is a tool-configuration excerpt, not a complete pyproject.toml. Merge it with package metadata, build requirements, dependency groups, and the xevolution = \"xevolution.cli:main\" script entry. See the official Ruff, mypy, and pytest guidance. Once the corresponding implementation exists, the standard checks are: uv sync --locked --group dev uv run --locked --group dev ruff format --check . uv run --locked --group dev ruff check . uv run --locked --group dev mypy src/xevolution uv run --locked --group dev python -m pytest uv build For formatting edits, use uv run --locked --group dev ruff format with the affected paths. Do not disable checks broadly or add blanket suppressions to hide failures. Use focused tests while developing; run the relevant broader checks before completing code changes. Documentation-only changes need proportionate documentation validation. CI should pin a reviewed uv version, record its selected Python versions, and use the committed lockfile. Each matrix job must select its interpreter explicitly through the python-version input of astral-sh/setup-uv or the UV_PYTHON environment variable. Do not rely on PATH alone to override the committed .python-version. See uv's CI guidance. Keep dependency upgrades intentional and separate from unrelated behavior changes when practical. 10. Testing expectations Test observable contracts and regressions, not private helper structure. Use small deterministic applications; do not require GPUs, network services, or long evolutionary runs for the default suite. Cover population size, parent/survivor distinctions, selection boundaries, finite retries, identity, age, evaluation coverage/accounting, and stopping. Include mutable nested genotypes and custom payloads that cannot be generically compared or hashed. Verify parent independence and historical snapshot integrity. Test both evaluation hooks, including jointly dependent population evaluation and invalid/duplicate/missing result identities. Compare uninterrupted execution with restoration in a new process at generation 0 and a later generation. Include stateful components and stopping, custom codecs, and failure during an uncommitted generation. Compare complete logical state and committed scientific history, not just the best fitness. Exercise real temporary files and targeted failure injection around journal and checkpoint operations. Test uncommitted-tail recovery separately from missing/corrupt committed history. Do not mock away the persistence layer being verified. Test that repeated analysis does not duplicate scientific records or alter evolutionary state. Avoid flaky statistical assertions and wall-clock sleeps. Use controlled random inputs and explicit boundary cases where practical. For packaging changes, build a wheel and smoke-test it outside the checkout in a disposable uv-created environment. Install that exact wheel using uv pip with the disposable interpreter explicitly selected, then invoke that interpreter directly. Verify import, the console entry point, python -m xevolution, and included py.typed/resources. A project-mode uv run that returns to the development environment would invalidate this check. Use the specification's acceptance criteria as the completion target. Add tests for changed behavior and concrete risks; avoid arbitrary coverage quotas or tests that merely repeat implementation details. 11. Agent workflow and completion Read the relevant specification sections, existing code, tests, and any instructions scoped to the files you will change. Preserve unrelated user work. Identify the behavior and state ownership affected. Use reasonable judgment for routine implementation choices within the requested scope. Implement a cohesive change. Keep documentation and examples synchronized with public behavior, configuration, and checkpoint formats. Record deliberate Java behavior corrections in docs/markdown/porting_notes.md. Verify the changed contract with the appropriate checks. Do not weaken the contract to make an existing test pass or claim unrun checks succeeded. Report what changed, why, what was verified, and any remaining limitations. Distinguish implementation, planned work, and environmental blockers clearly. For the initial port, follow the specification's sequence: in-memory evolution, reliable continuation, then headless tools and documentation. Establish state ownership and the completed-generation boundary before applications depend on the framework. Reference links The project policies above are specific to this port. Consult these primary references for tool behavior and Python semantics; version decisions belong in the repository configuration and lockfile.","order":14},{"title":"Maintain and rebuild the documentation","url":"building_docs.html","text":"Maintain and rebuild the documentation The documentation has two editions generated from one set of Markdown sources. The HTML pages are ready to read by opening docs/html/index.html in a browser. Reading requires no Python environment, web server, external stylesheet, or downloaded font. Directory layout Path within docs/ Purpose README.md Entry instructions for the downloadable documentation bundle markdown/index.md Markdown documentation home markdown/*.md Authoritative prose for the package, examples, and references markdown/verification.json Preserved machine-readable evidence from the verified Python 0.1.1 release markdown/resources/ Reference copies of the CI workflow and licensing notices html/index.html HTML documentation home; open this after extracting the ZIP html/*.html HTML pages generated from the corresponding Markdown files html/assets/ Local stylesheet, optional navigation/search code, and generated search index build_docs.py Standard-library Python driver for Pandoc conversion and link validation build_report.json Results and source identities recorded by the documentation build browser_verification.json Browser checks recorded for the delivered documentation edition The main documentation pages are flat within each format directory. For example, the Markdown link [checkpoints](checkpointing.md) becomes an HTML link to checkpointing.html. Section fragments are retained. Supporting resources are copied into the HTML tree at the same relative locations. Edit the source Change the relevant file under docs/markdown/, then rebuild. A correction to the HTML alone will be replaced on the next build. Use ordinary GitHub-flavored Markdown: headings, paragraphs, lists, fenced code blocks, and tables. Keep code fences labeled with their language, for example python, bash, or json. Use relative links between documentation pages. Link to another section using its heading fragment, and let the build validate that the target exists. Avoid links that depend on files outside the documentation bundle. When describing source code, name its path relative to the project root, such as examples/batch_problem.py. A documentation-only archive cannot resolve a clickable link to that external source file. Link to the relevant example guide when the explanation is what the reader needs. When adding a page, also add it to the grouped page catalog in build_docs.py and link to it from the documentation home or an appropriate guide. This keeps navigation and the generated search index complete. The port specification and coding guidelines are reference copies of the repository's xevolution_python_port_spec.md and AGENTS.md. Update the repository versions first when their requirements change, then refresh these copies and preserve their documentation introduction and local links. The reference workflow is a snapshot of .github/workflows/ci.yml. Build HTML Put this docs/ directory in the xevolution source project. Use its repository-local virtual environment managed by uv, and keep UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT unset or pointing to that .venv. Install the standalone Pandoc command-line tool using the method appropriate to your operating system. The documentation build was developed with Pandoc 3.1.3 and Python 3.12.14. The Python driver uses only the standard library; it adds no Python package dependency to xevolution. Pandoc is a separate executable, not a Python dependency. From the source project root: uv sync --locked --group dev pandoc --version uv run --locked python docs/build_docs.py If you already use the Digits example and want to retain its numerical stack, include all configured dependency groups on the same commands: uv sync --locked --all-groups uv run --locked --all-groups python docs/build_docs.py After a successful build, open docs/html/index.html. Keep the whole html/ directory together when copying it, including assets/ and supporting files. Keep its sibling markdown/ directory too if you want the HTML pages' Markdown source links to remain available. The builder regenerates HTML and local assets. It checks local file links, heading fragments, and duplicate HTML IDs; a missing document or broken local target is a build failure. External reference websites are not downloaded by the builder. The generated search works from local files and does not contact a service. To validate that the checked-in HTML still matches the Markdown and generator, without replacing the generated files, run: uv run --locked python docs/build_docs.py --check Add --all-groups after --locked when retaining the Digits dependencies. The check creates a temporary build, validates it, and compares the output files byte for byte with the existing HTML tree. Use the same Pandoc version when checking a previously generated edition. Check content as well as links A working link does not establish that an API example is correct. When editing commands or behavior, compare the guide with current code and configurations. Run a small relevant example where the change warrants it. For documentation changes, inspect representative HTML pages at desktop and narrow widths and check long tables and code blocks. The documentation release added full arithmetic and shared-resource guides, an introductory package overview, an examples comparison, and this maintenance guide. The actual arithmetic and shared-resource commands were checked again, including analysis and shared-resource pause/resume. The Digits results remain the measured results from the previously verified Python release. The release verification report and its JSON preserve that Python release's original tests, timing measurements, and artifact identities. Documentation edits do not imply that the full application test suite, wheel build, or hosted CI has run again. Documentation build results are recorded separately in docs/build_report.json. The delivered HTML was also opened directly from disk in Chromium at desktop (1440 × 1000) and phone (390 × 844) viewport sizes. All 16 pages were checked for a visible title, current-page navigation, working search initialization, and horizontal page overflow. Interaction checks covered local search, keyboard controls, section links, mobile navigation, and reading/navigation with JavaScript disabled. No browser errors or network requests occurred during these checks. Their results are retained in docs/browser_verification.json; the normal builder does not rerun those browser checks. Return to the documentation home or the package overview.","order":15}];
